QUESTIONS.push(
/* ---------------- Potency from curve shape ---------------- */
{id:"L14-001", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"potency", skill:"figure",
 concept:"potency-a-vs-b", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Compounds A and B are plotted for the same response against increasing dose. The response to A begins at a lower dose than the response to B. Which compound is more potent?",
 options:[
  {t:"A", correct:true, why:"The compound that produces the measured response at the lower dose is the more potent one."},
  {t:"B", correct:false, why:"B needs a higher dose before the response appears, so it is less potent."},
  {t:"Neither; potency needs a toxic dose", correct:false, why:"Potency compares the doses that produce the same response; no toxic dose is needed."},
  {t:"They are equally potent", correct:false, why:"The curves start at different doses."},
  {t:"It cannot be read without the LD50", correct:false, why:"LD50 is a separate value from a population mortality curve."}],
 teach:"Potency is read along the dose axis: for the same measured response, the compound that needs less dose to produce it is the more potent one. A starts producing the response at a lower dose than B, so A is more potent. The comparison is made at the same response, which is why no toxic or lethal value is needed.",
 quote:"I think you guys are able to tell me already which compound is most potent. A, right, because you start getting that effect, the response that we're measuring at a lower dose compared to B.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Potency comparison in Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-002", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"potency", skill:"figure",
 concept:"potency-c-vs-d-trick", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Compound C produces a response at lower doses than compound D, but as dose rises the response to C levels off while the response to D keeps rising linearly and crosses it. Which compound is more potent?",
 options:[
  {t:"C at low doses, D once the curves cross", correct:true, why:"Her answer: you cannot say C is always more potent; at low doses it is, and once C levels off and D's line crosses it, D is the more potent compound."},
  {t:"C, at every dose", correct:false, why:"C's response levels out with increasing dose, so above the crossing point D gives more response."},
  {t:"D, at every dose", correct:false, why:"At the lower doses C produces the response first."},
  {t:"Neither; the curves are for different responses", correct:false, why:"Both are plotted for the same response against dose."},
  {t:"D, as the straight line always shows the more potent compound", correct:false, why:"The shape of the line does not decide potency; the dose needed for a given response does."}],
 teach:"The right panel is her trick question. C starts responding at a lower dose than D, so at low doses C is more potent, but C's curve bends over while D's continues linearly across the doses tested, so after the lines cross D is more potent. Potency can change places with dose, which is why a study has to cover a wide enough range of doses to catch it.",
 quote:"So, you can't say that C is always more potent than D. You can in the lower doses, right? OK. But once you get to a certain point where it's leveled off and the line is crossing D's dose response line, you would say that D is more potent than C, correct?",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Potency comparison in Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-003", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"potency", skill:"apply", multi:true,
 concept:"why-curve-levels-off", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"The response to compound C levels off with increasing dose while compound D's keeps rising. Which of the following could explain why C's curve bends over? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Its receptors are fully saturated", correct:true, why:"The first class answer she accepted: all the binding sites are occupied and more dose gives no more response."},
  {t:"The enzymes that bioactivate it to an active metabolite are saturated", correct:true, why:"A kinetic explanation she offered: if C is bioactivated to something active, that metabolism can saturate."},
  {t:"The transporter that absorbs it across the gut, such as P-glycoprotein, is saturated", correct:true, why:"Her transport example: an oral compound that must be actively absorbed stops being moved once the transporter is saturated."},
  {t:"It changes the pH of the stomach as its dose rises", correct:true, why:"A student suggestion she accepted: a rising dose of C could change pH in the stomach and alter its own absorption."},
  {t:"D has a longer half-life than C", correct:false, why:"Half-life was not among the explanations; the question is why C's own response stops rising."}],
 teach:"Any step between dose and response that can be saturated will flatten C's curve. Once the receptors are fully saturated, once the metabolism that keeps C active can do no more turnover, or once a gut transporter such as P-glycoprotein is moving all it can, more dose adds no more response. She also accepted that C may hit the shared receptor differently than D and that rising C could change stomach pH. Target, binding, metabolism, distribution and absorption can each change potency with dose.",
 quote:"So we've talked about target, we've talked about tissue, we talked about, you know, where does the, where does the compound bind to its target. We talked about metabolism, we've talked about distribution, absorption issues. So all these things could be causing compound C's potency to change with an increasing dose, therefore, making this a trick question",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Potency comparison in Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-004", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"potency", skill:"apply",
 concept:"wide-dose-range", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A dose–response study of compounds C and D stops at a dose below the point where C's curve would level off. What does the study risk missing?",
 options:[
  {t:"That the potencies change places at higher doses", correct:true, why:"Her point: if the doses are cut off too early, nobody sees where C's curve is going and the reversal in potency is missed."},
  {t:"The threshold dose of D, which only the highest doses reveal", correct:false, why:"The threshold is at the low end of the curve, which the study did cover."},
  {t:"The LD50 of C", correct:false, why:"The study measures one response for C and D; cutting the doses short loses the high-dose part of C's curve, where it levels off and D crosses it, not a lethal-dose value."},
  {t:"That C is a toxin rather than a toxicant", correct:false, why:"Origin of the compound has nothing to do with the dose range."},
  {t:"Nothing, as the low doses are the only ones that matter", correct:false, why:"The high doses are where C and D cross."}],
 teach:"When designing a dose–response study, include a fairly large range of doses. If dosing stops too early, the curve of C has not yet bent over, and the fact that C and D switch places in potency is missed.",
 quote:"if we did not extend the doses far enough, we, like, let's say we cut off dosing, oh, about right here. And we weren't quite sure where the curve of C was going, we might miss the fact that their potency basically changes places, right?",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Potency comparison in Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

/* ---------------- ED, TD, LD curves ---------------- */
{id:"L14-005", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"edtdld", skill:"recall",
 concept:"ed-td-ld-order", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"On a graph of one compound with three dose–response lines, the lines from the lowest doses to the highest are the:",
 options:[
  {t:"Effective dose, toxic dose, lethal dose", correct:true, why:"Effective or therapeutic dose comes first, then a toxic response, then a lethal dose."},
  {t:"Toxic dose, effective dose, lethal dose", correct:false, why:"The therapeutic response appears before the toxic one."},
  {t:"Lethal dose, toxic dose, effective dose", correct:false, why:"Death is the last outcome to appear as dose rises, not the first; the therapeutic effect is what appears at the lowest doses, before toxicity and then lethality."},
  {t:"Effective dose, lethal dose, toxic dose", correct:false, why:"Toxicity precedes lethality as dose rises."},
  {t:"Threshold dose, effective dose, LD50", correct:false, why:"Threshold and LD50 are points, not the three curves on this slide."}],
 teach:"As dose rises, one compound can produce more than one kind of response. The first to appear is the intended one, the effective (therapeutic) dose, ED; at higher doses a toxic response appears, TD; and at higher doses still, death, LD. Each is its own dose-response line, and because the lines are not parallel, the mechanisms behind effect, toxicity and lethality can differ.",
 quote:"so effective or therapeutic dose is first, then a toxic response, and then a lethal dose. So again, this is increasing dose with increasing response, and we've got 3 lines, effective, toxic, or lethal.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘ED, TD and LD Comparison from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-006", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"edtdld", skill:"figure",
 concept:"nonparallel-different-mechanisms", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"The effective, toxic and lethal dose–response lines for one compound are not parallel. What does this suggest about the compound?",
 options:[
  {t:"Different targets and mechanisms produce its three outcomes", correct:true, why:"Her reading of the slide: non-parallel lines mean the mechanism producing effectiveness differs from the one producing toxicity and from the one producing death."},
  {t:"Its three outcomes are produced by one mechanism at one target", correct:false, why:"One mechanism at one target would be expected to give parallel lines."},
  {t:"The study mixed up three different compounds", correct:false, why:"It is one compound with increasing dose."},
  {t:"It has no threshold dose", correct:false, why:"The slope of the lines, not their starting point, was her point."},
  {t:"It is an essential nutrient", correct:false, why:"This slide is about three outcomes of one compound; whether the compound is a nutrient has nothing to do with why the lines are not parallel."}],
 teach:"Most people expect that increasing dose carries a compound linearly from effective to toxic to lethal, with parallel lines. When the three lines are not parallel, differences in ADME contribute, but the larger point is the last principle of toxicology: the targets and mechanisms that produce effectiveness, toxicity and death can be different, so the compound causes toxicity in a different way than it causes its effect.",
 quote:"if your dose response curves are not linear. Then it would lead you to believe that the mechanism for how this drug is being effective versus toxic versus lethal, that those mechanisms leading to those three particular outcomes are different, so it's causing toxicity in a different way. Than it's causing effectiveness",
 note:"She first accepted a student's answer, differences in ADME, then said the more important explanation is different targets and mechanisms.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘ED, TD and LD Comparison from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

/* ---------------- Indices: TI, MOS, MOE ---------------- */
{id:"L14-007", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"indices", skill:"recall",
 concept:"ti-convention", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The therapeutic index is conventionally calculated as:",
 options:[
  {t:"TD50 / ED50", correct:true, why:"The toxic dose in 50% of the animals over the therapeutic dose in 50% of the animals is the usual convention."},
  {t:"ED50 / TD50", correct:false, why:"Inverting the ratio loses its meaning: with the toxic dose on top, a wide index means the toxic dose sits well above the therapeutic dose and there is less chance of toxicity."},
  {t:"TD1 / ED99", correct:false, why:"That is the margin of safety."},
  {t:"NOAEL / exposure", correct:false, why:"That is the margin of exposure."},
  {t:"LD50 / threshold dose", correct:false, why:"The therapeutic index compares a toxic dose with a therapeutic (effective) dose read at the same percentage of animals; a threshold dose is not that effective dose."}],
 teach:"The therapeutic index asks how far above the therapeutic dose the toxic dose lies, so the toxic dose goes on top: usually the toxic dose in 50% of the animals over the therapeutic dose in 50%. A wide index means the toxic dose is well above the therapeutic dose and there is less chance of toxicity; a narrow one means it is not much higher. Another percentage can be used if numerator and denominator match.",
 quote:"most of the time we're using the toxic dose in 50% of the animals over the toxic dose, I'm sorry, over the therapeutic dose of 50% of the animals. That's usually the convention of how we do therapeutic index.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Values Developed from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-008", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"indices", skill:"recall",
 concept:"ti-same-percentage", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"If a therapeutic index is calculated with a percentage other than 50%, which rule applies?",
 options:[
  {t:"Numerator and denominator use the same percentage", correct:true, why:"Her rule: the percentage can differ from 50, but it must be the same for the toxic dose and the therapeutic dose."},
  {t:"The toxic dose must use 1% and the effective dose 99%", correct:false, why:"That pairing of different percentages defines the margin of safety, not the therapeutic index."},
  {t:"The percentage must be higher for the toxic dose", correct:false, why:"Unequal percentages turn the ratio into a margin-of-safety type of value; for the therapeutic index the toxic and therapeutic doses are read at the same percentage of animals."},
  {t:"Only LD values may replace TD values", correct:false, why:"The rule is about percentages, not about swapping lethal for toxic doses."},
  {t:"The index must then be called a margin of exposure", correct:false, why:"The margin of exposure uses the NOAEL and an exposure, not percentages of animals."}],
 teach:"The therapeutic index compares the toxic and therapeutic curves at one matching point, conventionally the dose that affects 50% of the animals on each. Another percentage can be used, but the numerator and denominator must use the same one. Using different percentages, the toxic dose for 1% over the effective dose for 99%, is what defines the margin of safety and brings in the slope of the curve.",
 quote:"It doesn't always have to be 50%. It could be a different percentage, but the numerator and the denominator need to have the same percentage, OK.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Values Developed from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-009", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"indices", skill:"tell", dupOf:"L13-032",
 concept:"ti-mos-moe", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which value gives an idea of the slope of the dose–response curve, and why?",
 options:[
  {t:"Margin of safety: two different percentages, TD1 and ED99", correct:true, why:"Her words: the margin of safety is the toxic dose for 1% of the animals over the effective dose of 99%, and that difference in percentage is what shows the slope."},
  {t:"The therapeutic index, as it uses TD50 and ED50", correct:false, why:"The therapeutic index looks at one point on each graph and gives no slope information."},
  {t:"The margin of exposure, as it divides the NOAEL by a measured chronic exposure", correct:false, why:"The margin of exposure compares a no-effect dose with a measured exposure; it is not a slope measure."},
  {t:"The LD50, as it comes from a population curve", correct:false, why:"LD50 is one point at 50% mortality."},
  {t:"None of them; slope needs a graph", correct:false, why:"The margin of safety was built to carry slope information."}],
 teach:"The margin of safety can be used for a therapeutic compound, like the therapeutic index, but it changes the percentages: the toxic dose for 1% of the animals over the effective dose for 99%. Using a different percentage in the numerator and the denominator gives an idea of the slope of the dose–response curve, which the therapeutic index does not.",
 quote:"most of the time, a margin of safety number is developed because we say what's the toxic dose for 1% of the animals over the effective dose of 99% of the animals. That's the margin of safety. And because we use a different percentage, it gives you an idea of the slope of your dose response curve, whereas the therapeutic index does not.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Values Developed from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-010", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"indices", skill:"recall", dupOf:"L13-033",
 concept:"moe-repeated", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The margin of exposure is the value used for:",
 options:[
  {t:"Repeated exposures to a non-therapeutic compound", correct:true, why:"Her description: used truly in toxicology, for something that is not therapeutic, such as a pollutant or an occupational exposure, and for repeated exposures."},
  {t:"A single dose of a medication", correct:false, why:"Single doses of medications use the therapeutic index or the margin of safety."},
  {t:"Reading the slope of a therapeutic curve", correct:false, why:"That is the margin of safety."},
  {t:"Comparing two medications' potencies at one chosen response", correct:false, why:"Potency compares doses for one response; the margin of exposure compares a no-effect dose with an exposure."},
  {t:"Deciding the LD50 of a pesticide", correct:false, why:"LD50 is read from a mortality curve."}],
 teach:"Medications have a therapeutic dose to compare against, but a pollutant or occupational chemical does not, and people meet it over and over. So the margin of exposure puts the NOAEL, a dose that caused no adverse response in animal studies, over the chronic exposure people (or wildlife) are calculated to receive. Margins for different contaminants can then be compared to judge which is more dangerous.",
 quote:"the margin of exposure, this is something that we use truly in toxicology with exposures to something that's not therapeutic, like to, you know, a pollutant, an occupational exposure, OK, and it's also for repeated exposures, all right?",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Values Developed from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-011", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"indices", skill:"recall",
 concept:"noael-definition", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the margin of exposure, the numerator is the NOAEL. Where does the NOAEL come from and what is it?",
 options:[
  {t:"Animal studies: a dose at which the animals had no adverse response", correct:true, why:"The no observable adverse effect level is generated in animal exposure studies of the compound."},
  {t:"Human surveys: the dose that exposed people report tolerating without symptoms", correct:false, why:"The NOAEL is an animal-study value; the human side is the exposure in the denominator."},
  {t:"The population mortality curve: the LD50", correct:false, why:"The NOAEL is a dose with no adverse effect, not a lethal dose."},
  {t:"The therapeutic curve: the ED99", correct:false, why:"ED99 belongs to the margin of safety."},
  {t:"Water sampling: the measured concentration", correct:false, why:"Measured exposure is the denominator of the margin of exposure."}],
 teach:"NOAEL stands for no observable adverse effect level (she pronounces it \"Noel\"). It is a dose used in animal exposure studies of the compound at which the exposed animals did not have an adverse response. It goes over the exposure that humans, or a wild animal of concern, are thought to be getting: the exposure is calculated as the total exposure the person has received.",
 quote:"it stands for the no observable adverse effect level. OK, so no no observable adverse effect level. That is a dose that we used in animal exposure studies for this compound. That the animals that we exposed did not have an adverse response.",
 note:"She then phrased it as \"what's the lowest dose we gave the animals that we were testing with this compound and they didn't have a bad response to?\" The existing glossary entry (margin-of-exposure) describes it as the highest dose that caused no adverse effect; the question avoids lowest/highest and keys what both agree on.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Values Developed from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-012", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"indices", skill:"apply",
 concept:"moe-denominator", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A risk assessor calculates a margin of exposure for a contaminant at a polluted site. What goes in the denominator?",
 options:[
  {t:"The total exposure the people at the site are estimated to have received", correct:true, why:"The denominator is the exposure, calculated as how much total exposure a human, or a critter in the wild, has gotten to the compound."},
  {t:"The NOAEL from the animal study", correct:false, why:"The NOAEL is the numerator."},
  {t:"The effective dose in 99% of animals from the same animal study as the NOAEL", correct:false, why:"ED99 is the denominator of the margin of safety, not the margin of exposure."},
  {t:"The LD50 in rats", correct:false, why:"Lethality is not part of the margin of exposure."},
  {t:"The therapeutic dose of the compound", correct:false, why:"A pollutant has no therapeutic dose; that is why the margin of exposure exists."}],
 teach:"Margin of exposure = NOAEL over the chronic exposure. The NOAEL is generated in animal exposure studies; the denominator is the exposure, which the assessor calculates as the total exposure a human (or an animal in the wild) has received to the compound.",
 quote:"the Noel is what we generate in animal exposure studies, and the denominator of exposure is we calculate, we try to figure out how much total exposure has a human gotten to that compound. OK, so that's the margin of exposure.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Values Developed from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-013", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"indices", skill:"apply",
 concept:"indices-compare-compounds", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A polluted site holds several contaminants and the cleanup budget covers only one. How do the margins of exposure help decide which to clean up?",
 options:[
  {t:"Compare the values and clean up the more dangerous compound", correct:true, why:"The real value of these numbers is comparing one compound's value with another's to estimate how bad each is."},
  {t:"Clean up the compound with the highest NOAEL", correct:false, why:"A high NOAEL by itself means the animals tolerated a high dose; the ratio with exposure is what matters."},
  {t:"Clean up the compound present at the highest concentration in the soil samples", correct:false, why:"Concentration alone ignores how much exposure it takes to cause harm."},
  {t:"The margin of exposure cannot be used for pollutants", correct:false, why:"Pollutants are exactly what the margin of exposure is for."},
  {t:"Use the therapeutic index of each contaminant instead", correct:false, why:"Pollutants have no therapeutic dose."}],
 teach:"Each margin of exposure sets a contaminant's animal no-adverse-effect dose (NOAEL) against the exposure people at the site actually receive, so it reflects both how toxic the compound is and how much of it people get. A single value means little alone; its use is comparison. Calculating it for each contaminant shows which is more dangerous to the people exposed, and that is where limited cleanup money goes.",
 quote:"You'd calculate that margin of exposure and figure out which compound is more dangerous to humans being exposed to it. And therefore you might decide to spend your money to clean up that more dangerous compound if you had to make a choice on what to clean up.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Values Developed from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-014", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"indices", skill:"tell",
 concept:"wide-vs-narrow-ti", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Compared with a drug with a wide therapeutic index, a drug with a narrow therapeutic index:",
 options:[
  {t:"Has a toxic dose close to its therapeutic dose", correct:true, why:"Her definition of narrow: the toxic dose is not too much higher than the therapeutic dose, so there is more chance of toxicity."},
  {t:"Has less chance of toxicity", correct:false, why:"Less chance of toxicity belongs to the wide therapeutic index."},
  {t:"Allows more error in dosing", correct:false, why:"A wide index is the one that tolerates dosing error."},
  {t:"Has a steeper effective-dose curve than the wide-index drug", correct:false, why:"The therapeutic index carries no slope information."},
  {t:"Is non-therapeutic", correct:false, why:"A therapeutic index is calculated for medications."}],
 teach:"A medication with a wide therapeutic index has less chance of toxicity; one with a narrow index has a toxic dose that is not much higher than its therapeutic dose. The therapeutic index tells how much error can be tolerated in dosing. The same comparison logic applies to margins of safety and margins of exposure.",
 quote:"less chance of toxicity for the drug with a wide therapeutic index. Drugs with a narrow therapeutic index means that the toxic dose is not too much higher than that therapeutic dose, right?",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Values Developed from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-015", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"indices", skill:"recall", type:"match",
 concept:"three-indices-table", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Match each value developed from dose–response curves with its formula.",
 left:["Therapeutic index", "Margin of safety", "Margin of exposure"],
 right:["TD50 / ED50", "TD1 / ED99", "NOAEL / chronic exposure"],
 pairs:[
  {l:"Therapeutic index", r:"TD50 / ED50", why:"Toxic dose over therapeutic dose, conventionally at 50%; one point on each graph, no slope information."},
  {l:"Margin of safety", r:"TD1 / ED99", why:"Different percentages in numerator and denominator, so the value reflects the slope."},
  {l:"Margin of exposure", r:"NOAEL / chronic exposure", why:"The animal no-adverse-effect dose over the calculated human or wildlife exposure; for repeated, non-therapeutic exposures."}],
 teach:"Three numbers come from dose–response curves. The therapeutic index (TD50 / ED50) and the margin of safety (TD1 / ED99) apply to therapeutic compounds and single doses; the margin of safety also shows slope. The margin of exposure (NOAEL / chronic exposure) applies to non-therapeutic compounds and repeated exposures. All three are most useful for comparing compounds with each other.",
 quote:"therapeutic index is looking at efficacy compared to, compared to toxicity, usually using 50% of animals, just looking at one point in the graph ... we're using like a toxic dose in 1% of the animals compared to the effective dose in 99% ... But our margin of exposure is for non-therapeutic compounds with repeated exposures",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Values Developed from Dose-Response’; transcript 10/13"},

/* ---------------- Target and mechanism of action ---------------- */
{id:"L14-016", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"target", skill:"apply", dupOf:"L13-034",
 concept:"target-not-highest-concentration", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A compound damages the kidney. Tissue sampling shows its concentration is highest in muscle, where there is no damage. Which statement is consistent with the target-and-mechanism principle?",
 options:[
  {t:"The kidney is more sensitive; harm need not occur where concentration is highest", correct:true, why:"Her example: higher concentration in muscle with no adverse effect there, damage in the kidney at a lower concentration, which is counterintuitive."},
  {t:"The muscle concentration must have been measured wrongly", correct:false, why:"Nothing is wrong with the measurement; the organs differ in sensitivity."},
  {t:"Damage always occurs where the concentration is highest, so the kidney level was misread", correct:false, why:"That is the intuition she said is wrong."},
  {t:"The kidney concentration must exceed the muscle concentration", correct:false, why:"In the example the kidney concentration is the lower one."},
  {t:"The compound has only one target", correct:false, why:"The number of targets is a separate point; this one is about sensitivity versus concentration."}],
 teach:"Where a compound has its adverse effect may not be where it reaches its highest concentration. It may be concentrated in muscle with no effect there and damage the kidney at a lower concentration if the kidney is more sensitive. Even so, the dose at the target still determines the toxicity: a super-sensitive kidney is not harmed unless the concentration there gets high enough, and there is still a dose–response curve at the kidney.",
 quote:"So where it's having an adverse effect may not be where the highest concentration is, which is probably a little counterintuitive, right? ... not necessarily, maybe the kidneys like just super sensitive. Compared to the muscle.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Target & Mechanism of Action’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-017", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"target", skill:"recall",
 concept:"dose-at-target-sensitive-organ", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The kidney is the most sensitive organ to a compound. Under what condition does the compound damage the kidney?",
 options:[
  {t:"Only when its concentration in the kidney gets high enough", correct:true, why:"Even a super-sensitive site has a dose–response curve; the dose at the target determines the toxicity."},
  {t:"At any dose, as sensitivity removes the need for a threshold", correct:false, why:"Sensitivity lowers the dose needed, but the concentration at the kidney still has to be high enough."},
  {t:"Only when the kidney is also the site of highest concentration", correct:false, why:"The site of harm need not be the site of highest concentration."},
  {t:"Only after the muscle is damaged first", correct:false, why:"In her example the muscle had the highest concentration and no damage at all; kidney harm depends on the concentration that reaches the kidney."},
  {t:"Only with repeated exposure", correct:false, why:"Frequency was not the point; the dose at the target was."}],
 teach:"The dose at the place where the compound acts determines toxicity. A sensitive organ is still unharmed if the compound does not reach a high enough concentration there. There is still a dose–response curve right at the kidney.",
 quote:"let's say the kidney is super sensitive, but if that compound doesn't get high enough in the kidney, it may not cause a problem to the kidney. So even the super sensitive places, the concentration has to be high enough to cause an effect. Remember, you still have a dose response curve even right there at the kidney.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Target & Mechanism of Action’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-018", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"target", skill:"apply",
 concept:"multiple-targets-different-mechanisms", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A compound damages both the kidney and the liver. What does the target-and-mechanism principle say about how the damage happens?",
 options:[
  {t:"The kidney and liver mechanisms are likely different", correct:true, why:"With multiple targets, the compound is likely causing damage to the two organs in different ways: one mechanism in the kidney, one in the liver."},
  {t:"The same mechanism must operate in both organs", correct:false, why:"Different targets usually mean different mechanisms."},
  {t:"Only the organ with the higher concentration is truly a target", correct:false, why:"Both are targets; concentration does not decide which organ is harmed."},
  {t:"All of its targets have been identified", correct:false, why:"She said we may not have identified all the places a compound hits."},
  {t:"Its therapeutic target must be one of the two organs", correct:false, why:"Toxic targets can differ from the therapeutic target."}],
 teach:"When one compound hits several targets, each target is affected in its own way: damage to the kidney likely comes from one mechanism and damage to the liver from another. The non-parallel effective, toxic and lethal curves show the same idea, since different slopes point to different mechanisms. Finding two damaged organs also does not mean every target is known; some may not yet be identified.",
 quote:"let's say the compound affects the kidney and the liver. It's likely to be causing damage to those two different organs in different ways. One mechanism in the kidney, one mechanism in the liver, OK.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Target & Mechanism of Action’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-019", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"target", skill:"tell",
 concept:"side-effect-therapy-too-far-vs-other-target", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Over-diuresis with a diuretic and ototoxicity with an aminoglycoside are both adverse effects. What separates them?",
 options:[
  {t:"Over-diuresis is therapy gone too far; ototoxicity comes from an unrelated target", correct:true, why:"Her two examples: diuretics taking too much fluid off are therapy gone too far; aminoglycoside ototoxicity is an adverse effect whose target differs from the therapeutic target and mechanism."},
  {t:"Both are the therapeutic action gone too far", correct:false, why:"Killing bacteria has nothing to do with damage to hearing."},
  {t:"Both come from targets unrelated to the therapeutic one, so neither depends on the therapeutic dose", correct:false, why:"Dehydration from a diuretic is its therapeutic mechanism overshooting."},
  {t:"Over-diuresis is irreversible; ototoxicity is reversible", correct:false, why:"Reversibility was not the distinction she drew between these examples."},
  {t:"Only ototoxicity is dose-related", correct:false, why:"Both depend on dose; the difference is which target produces them."}],
 teach:"Many side effects in a drug list have nothing to do with the drug's therapeutic action, and the target-and-mechanism principle is the reason. Some side effects are the therapy gone too far: a diuretic that removes so much fluid the patient needs rehydration. Others, such as aminoglycosides causing ototoxicity, come from a target of the adverse effect that is very different from the therapeutic target and mechanism. Sometimes the mechanism of an adverse effect is known or can be found; sometimes it is not known.",
 quote:"Classic example would be diuretics ... that would be kind of a classic example of a side effect of diuretics where the therapy has gone too far ... why doivomar drugs like our amino glycosides cause ootoxicity. That's weird stuff ... the classic example of how the target of an adverse effect could be very different than the therapeutic targeting mechanism.",
 cite:"Principles-of-Tox-Lecture-Students-Fall-2026_WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Target & Mechanism of Action’; transcript 10/13"},

/* ---------------- The reading, as discussed ---------------- */
{id:"L14-020", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"reading", skill:"recall", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz",
 concept:"reading-who-met", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"The Rodriguez-Mozaz and Weinberg article reports on a 2008 environmental health summit. What was the subject of the meeting, and who attended?",
 options:[
  {t:"Drugs contaminating water; government agencies such as the EPA, academics, drug industry and other scientists", correct:true, why:"Her summary of the answers she accepted: an environmental health summit with government organizations such as the EPA, academicians, the pharmacy industry and other scientists interested in the health of the planet."},
  {t:"Antibiotic dosing in livestock feedlots and chicken houses; veterinarians, farmers and the agencies that regulate them", correct:false, why:"Livestock came up as one source, not as the subject of the summit."},
  {t:"Drinking-water treatment technology; water utilities only", correct:false, why:"Treatment methods were the part of the paper she did not assign."},
  {t:"Pharmacogenomics; geneticists", correct:false, why:"Pharmacogenomics is a later lecture, not this paper."},
  {t:"Risk assessment of a single polluted site; one state agency", correct:false, why:"The meeting was about drugs in the environment broadly, not one site."}],
 teach:"The article describes an environmental health summit in 2008, the first time these groups met about the finding that drugs are contaminating the environment, particularly water. Government organizations such as the EPA, academics, the pharmaceutical industry and other scientists interested in the planet's health attended. Their purpose was to decide what is known, how bad the problem is, and what to do; the part on what to do (research and educating scientists and the public) was not assigned.",
 quote:"it was an environmental health summit. Who was there? ... government organizations of various kinds, EPA, other agencies that, uh, might be worried about the environment, uh, academicians. Uh, pharmacy industry, right? Other scientists that have an interest in our health of our planet",
 note:"She first said 2018, then corrected herself to 2008.",
 cite:"Discussion-Guide-Rodriguez-Mozaz-and-Weinberg-Student-2026 copy.pdf; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-021", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"reading", skill:"recall", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz",
 concept:"reading-why-assigned", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Why does a pharmacy student need to know about pharmaceuticals in the water supply?",
 options:[
  {t:"Patients who hear about it will ask whether the water is dangerous", correct:true, why:"Her second reason for assigning the paper: patients read or hear a podcast about drugs in the water and come to the pharmacist worried about drinking someone else's Prozac."},
  {t:"Pharmacists are required to test the tap water in their community for drugs", correct:false, why:"Water testing was done by the scientists after the summit, not by pharmacists."},
  {t:"The drugs in water replace the need for prescriptions", correct:false, why:"Concentrations are far below therapeutic doses."},
  {t:"It is required for the pharmacy licensing examination", correct:false, why:"Her two reasons were about the subject itself: the paper is a way to explore the principles of toxicology, and patients will come in worried about drugs in their water."},
  {t:"Drinking water is the main route of drug overdose", correct:false, why:"Drugs in water are at parts per trillion, nowhere near a therapeutic daily dose, so the issue is an unwanted low-level exposure, not overdose."}],
 teach:"She assigned the paper for two reasons: it is a good way to explore the principles of toxicology, and patients will come to the pharmacist worried after reading or hearing that drugs are in the water supply. The goal is to be able to tell that patient whether to calm down or be concerned.",
 quote:"they will find out that we know that there's drugs in our water supply, and they might likely come to you freaking out ... We're going to get you to the point where you can talk to your patient that came to you freaking out, and you can help them calm down or be concerned.",
 cite:"Discussion-Guide-Rodriguez-Mozaz-and-Weinberg-Student-2026 copy.pdf; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-022", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"reading", skill:"recall", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz",
 concept:"reading-water-types", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which kinds of water does the discussion of pharmaceuticals in water cover?",
 options:[
  {t:"Surface water, groundwater and drinking-water sources", correct:true, why:"The three she defined before starting: lakes, streams and rivers; groundwater such as the Edwards Aquifer; and the sources drinking water is drawn from."},
  {t:"Ocean water only", correct:false, why:"The waters she defined sit where people drink from and wildlife live: lakes, streams and rivers, aquifers, and drinking-water sources; the ocean was not one of the three."},
  {t:"Hospital wastewater and the effluent of pharmaceutical factories only", correct:false, why:"The paper looks at the waters people and wildlife are exposed to."},
  {t:"Bottled water", correct:false, why:"She defined the waters by where they sit in the environment: surface water, groundwater and the sources drinking water is pulled from, not a packaged product."},
  {t:"Rainwater", correct:false, why:"Her three waters are where drugs accumulate after use: surface water receiving treated wastewater, groundwater, and drinking-water sources; rain was not one of them."}],
 teach:"Before applying the principles, she defined the waters where drugs end up and where people and animals meet them: surface water (lakes, streams, rivers), groundwater (such as the Edwards Aquifer) and the sources drinking water is pulled from. Treated wastewater still carrying drugs flows back into the surface water, which is how the drugs reach these waters.",
 quote:"we're talking about surface water, we're talking about groundwater, and we're talking about drinking water sources.",
 cite:"Discussion-Guide-Rodriguez-Mozaz-and-Weinberg-Student-2026 copy.pdf; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-023", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"reading", skill:"apply", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz", multi:true,
 concept:"reading-sources", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"both",
 stem:"Applying the source principle to pharmaceuticals in water: which of the following are sources she named? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Unmetabolized drug and metabolites excreted by people who take medications", correct:true, why:"People take drugs, go to the bathroom and flush; parent drug and metabolites both come out."},
  {t:"Improper disposal of unused medications down the toilet", correct:true, why:"Her example: unused antibiotics dumped down the toilet."},
  {t:"Livestock on feedlots and in chicken houses given hormones and antibiotics", correct:true, why:"Animal waste from medicated livestock is a source."},
  {t:"Wastewater treatment plants whose current processes do not remove all drugs", correct:true, why:"Treated water goes back to streams, rivers and lakes with drugs still in it; a source for both humans and animals."},
  {t:"Pharmaceutical manufacturing discharges", correct:false, why:"Her sources all trace drugs after use: what people excrete or flush, what medicated livestock excrete, and what treatment plants fail to remove; manufacturing was not on her list."}],
 teach:"Each source is a path from a drug's use to the water. People take medications and flush away parent drug and metabolites; unused medications are dumped down the toilet; medicated livestock on feedlots and in chicken houses add hormones and antibiotics in their waste. All of it reaches wastewater, and treatment plants do not remove all the drugs before the water returns to streams, rivers and lakes, so the same sources reach humans and wildlife.",
 quote:"We take the drugs, we flush, we improperly dispose of our meds. We've got animals on feedlots. Our wastewater treatment plants don't take care of all the drugs before it gets released into the environment, right? So that's the source for our exposure.",
 cite:"Discussion-Guide-Rodriguez-Mozaz-and-Weinberg-Student-2026 copy.pdf (Source hint); transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-024", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"reading", skill:"tell", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz", dupOf:"L13-035",
 concept:"reading-source-wwtp", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Wastewater treatment plants release water that still contains drugs. For whom is this a source of exposure?",
 options:[
  {t:"Both humans and wildlife", correct:true, why:"The answer she accepted: the treated water returns to streams, rivers and lakes, so it is a source for both."},
  {t:"Wildlife only", correct:false, why:"Drinking-water sources draw on the same surface water and groundwater."},
  {t:"Humans only", correct:false, why:"Aquatic animals live in the receiving streams and lakes."},
  {t:"Neither; treatment removes the drugs", correct:false, why:"Current treatment does not get all the drugs out."},
  {t:"Livestock only", correct:false, why:"Livestock are a source, not the group exposed by the plants."}],
 teach:"The water from flushing is treated, but current treatment mechanisms do not get all the drugs out before the water returns to streams, rivers and lakes. That makes the treatment plant a source of exposure for humans and for animals in the wild alike.",
 quote:"Wastewater treatment plants, yeah, the water from Flushing gets treated a bit, but right now the current mechanisms don't get all the drugs out of it before it goes back into your streams and rivers and lakes, OK. So is that a source for humans or animals? Both, there you go.",
 cite:"Discussion-Guide-Rodriguez-Mozaz-and-Weinberg-Student-2026 copy.pdf (Source hint); transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-025", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"reading", skill:"tell", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz",
 concept:"reading-exposure-humans-vs-wildlife", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"both",
 stem:"How does the route of exposure to drugs in water differ between humans and aquatic wildlife?",
 options:[
  {t:"Humans mostly by drinking; wildlife by drinking, breathing, skin and eating", correct:true, why:"Her summary: for us it is usually drinking water; animals in or near water are exposed constantly by drinking, breathing (fish), skin (frogs) and eating. Her key: humans by ingestion of drinking water, ingestion of fish and recreational dermal exposure; wildlife by ingestion of drinking water, ingestion of food and dermal; aquatic wildlife exposure is the more significant."},
  {t:"Both mostly by drinking", correct:false, why:"Animals living in the water are exposed by several routes at once."},
  {t:"Humans by skin while bathing or swimming in a lake; wildlife by drinking only", correct:false, why:"Bathing and swimming are possible human routes, but drinking is the main one."},
  {t:"Humans by inhalation; wildlife by injection", correct:false, why:"Human exposure is mostly through drinking water; wildlife are exposed by living in the water (drinking, breathing through gills, skin, eating), not by being injected."},
  {t:"Neither is exposed; the drugs stay in sediment", correct:false, why:"Drugs were measured in drinking water at parts per trillion, and animals in the water are exposed constantly, so both groups are exposed."}],
 teach:"Exposure: humans are mostly exposed by drinking water (bathing or swimming in a lake are possible). Animals that live in or near water are exposed constantly: drinking, breathing through gills, absorption across skin (frogs absorb a lot and are a sentinel species), and eating other animals that live in the water. Her key adds that wildlife exposure lasts their entire life, is repeated, and is constant if their home range is continually contaminated.",
 quote:"so they get exposed drinking, eating, swimming in the water ... the route of exposure, drinking, eating, swimming, living in it, OK? So that's our second principle.",
 note:"Breathing (gills) is from her 10/13 speech; her key (KeyDiscussionGuideRodriguez-MozazandWeinberg2026.docx) lists drinking water, food and dermal for wildlife.",
 cite:"Discussion-Guide-Rodriguez-Mozaz-and-Weinberg-Student-2026 copy.pdf (Exposure hint); KeyDiscussionGuideRodriguez-MozazandWeinberg2026.docx (Exposure); transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-026", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"reading", skill:"recall", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz",
 concept:"reading-ppt-concentration", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"When drinking water was tested, the concentration of most medications was in the range of:",
 options:[
  {t:"Parts per trillion", correct:true, why:"The answer she accepted; rarely is it higher."},
  {t:"Parts per million", correct:false, why:"Far higher than what was found."},
  {t:"Milligrams per liter", correct:false, why:"Far higher than what was found."},
  {t:"Therapeutic concentrations", correct:false, why:"The levels do not approach a therapeutic dose."},
  {t:"Undetectable", correct:false, why:"The drugs were detected, at parts per trillion."}],
 teach:"Most drugs tested in drinking water were found at about parts per trillion, rarely higher. That number drives her answer on dose: even a well-hydrated person drinking that water does not get near a normal therapeutic daily dose. Local levels can run higher; estrogen is higher along the shorelines of big cities where many young women may be taking oral contraceptives.",
 quote:"remember the concentrations of these drugs is found to be about parts per trillion. Rarely is it higher than that.",
 cite:"Discussion-Guide-Rodriguez-Mozaz-and-Weinberg-Student-2026 copy.pdf (Dose); transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-027", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"reading", skill:"tell", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz",
 concept:"reading-adme-known-humans-not-wildlife", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"both",
 stem:"For the drugs found in water, how does our knowledge of ADME, dose and target compare between humans and wild animals?",
 options:[
  {t:"Known for humans except many metabolites; largely unknown for wildlife", correct:true, why:"Drug development studies established ADME, dose and mechanism in humans, but not for unknown metabolites, and test species are rodents, dogs and pigs, not otters, whales, fish or mollusks."},
  {t:"Known equally well for both", correct:false, why:"Wild species are not used in drug development."},
  {t:"Unknown for both", correct:false, why:"Human data come from the studies done to approve the drugs."},
  {t:"Known for wildlife from field studies of fish and frogs; unknown for humans", correct:false, why:"Human data are the ones that exist, from drug development studies; wild species such as fish are not used in those studies, so their ADME, dose and targets are largely unknown."},
  {t:"Known for humans including every metabolite", correct:false, why:"Many metabolites are unknown and have no data."}],
 teach:"ADME, dose and target: for humans, drug development studies established how these drugs work, their ADME and their dose, but many metabolites are unknown and have no data. For wildlife we rarely know any of it: the animals used in drug development are rodents, guinea pigs, dogs and pigs, not sea otters, orca whales, fish or mollusks, so we do not know how a drug made and tested for humans will act in those animals.",
 quote:"we don't tend to use sea otters and orca whales and fish and mollusks. We don't use those in our drug development studies, so we don't know how these drugs made for humans and tested for humans are going to act. In these critters",
 cite:"Discussion-Guide-Rodriguez-Mozaz-and-Weinberg-Student-2026 copy.pdf (ADME hints); transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-028", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"reading", skill:"apply", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz",
 concept:"reading-patient-answer", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A well-hydrated patient heard a podcast about drugs in the water supply and asks whether drinking tap water is giving them a dose of someone else's antidepressant. What is the answer she gave?",
 options:[
  {t:"Probably OK: at parts per trillion the intake does not approach a therapeutic dose", correct:true, why:"Her conclusion: for humans we can be OK; the concentration does not get a person up to a normal therapeutic daily dose."},
  {t:"The patient should stop drinking tap water", correct:false, why:"Because intake at parts per trillion stays far below a normal therapeutic daily dose, she told the patient they are probably OK; avoiding tap water was not needed."},
  {t:"The patient is receiving a full dose daily", correct:false, why:"Parts per trillion are far below a therapeutic dose."},
  {t:"Nothing is known about human risk at these concentrations, so the patient should worry", correct:false, why:"Some data exist from drug development studies, and the dose is probably below a therapeutic dose. Her key does list open questions for humans: the effects of low-dose chronic exposure, sensitive populations and multiple exposures."},
  {t:"The water should be boiled to remove the drugs", correct:false, why:"Her reassurance rested on dose, not on treating water at home: parts per trillion does not approach a therapeutic daily dose. Removal is a question for wastewater and drinking-water treatment plants."}],
 teach:"Dose: at parts per trillion, even a well-hydrated person drinking contaminated water does not reach a normal therapeutic daily dose, so the patient is probably OK. Sensitive populations (in utero, children, the elderly, people with diseases, the immunocompromised) should not be ignored, and if concentrations start rising the risk assessors will re-evaluate; but her former work in risk assessment has not found that these levels are a problem for humans. Her key lists what is still unknown for humans: the effects of low-dose chronic exposures, good data on sensitive populations, and how multiple exposures act.",
 quote:"is that gonna get you up to a normal therapeutic daily dose? No, no ... So I think for humans we can be OK. Doesn't mean we should ignore our sensitive populations, which are in utero, elderly kids, people with diseases.",
 cite:"Discussion-Guide-Rodriguez-Mozaz-and-Weinberg-Student-2026 copy.pdf (Dose); KeyDiscussionGuideRodriguez-MozazandWeinberg2026.docx (ADME, Dose, Target/MOA); transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-029", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"reading", skill:"recall", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz", multi:true,
 concept:"reading-sensitive-populations", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which groups did she name as the sensitive populations to keep watching for exposure to drugs in drinking water? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"The fetus in utero", correct:true, why:"The developing child is exposed through the mother, and she said risk assessment for in utero exposure is very hard to do, so it is watched even though no harm has been shown."},
  {t:"The elderly", correct:true, why:"Part of her sensitive group with in utero, kids and people with diseases: not to be ignored, and her key says good data on them are lacking."},
  {t:"Children", correct:true, why:"Part of her sensitive group with in utero, the elderly and people with diseases: not to be ignored, and her key says good data on them are lacking."},
  {t:"People with disease states or who are immunocompromised", correct:true, why:"A student offered the immunocompromised and disease states and she accepted them; people with diseases are in her sensitive group, for whom good data are lacking."},
  {t:"Athletes who drink large volumes of water", correct:false, why:"She used the well-hydrated person to show the dose is still far below therapeutic."}],
 teach:"Even though humans are probably OK, sensitive populations should not be ignored: in utero, children, the elderly, people with diseases and the immunocompromised. Risk assessment on in utero exposure is very hard to do, and no articles show a child affected in utero by drugs in the water supply.",
 quote:"Maybe people that are immunocompromised, maybe with some disease states. Young in utero, right? ... Doesn't mean we should ignore our sensitive populations, which are in utero, elderly kids, people with diseases.",
 cite:"Discussion-Guide-Rodriguez-Mozaz-and-Weinberg-Student-2026 copy.pdf (Dose); transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-030", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"reading", skill:"apply", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz", dupOf:"L13-036",
 concept:"reading-wildlife-effects", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"Which observation in wildlife did she give as evidence that drugs in water are harming animal populations?",
 options:[
  {t:"Frogs, some fish and alligators shifting toward female where estrogen is higher", correct:true, why:"Her example: populations changing from about half male, half female to more female in places with higher estrogen."},
  {t:"Fish growing larger and living longer downstream of the wastewater treatment plants", correct:false, why:"The effect she described was on sex ratio, not growth: frog, fish and alligator populations shifting toward female where estrogen levels are higher."},
  {t:"Orca whales with measured drug levels", correct:false, why:"She said orcas are hard to catch and study; we do not know."},
  {t:"Birds dying after eating treated cattle", correct:false, why:"Her evidence came from animals living in the water (frogs, some fish, alligators) shifting toward female where estrogen is high; medicated livestock were a source of drugs, not a harmed population."},
  {t:"Livestock becoming sterile", correct:false, why:"Livestock were a source, not an affected population."}],
 teach:"Target and dose in wildlife: where estrogen levels are higher, populations of frogs, some fish and alligators shift from about half male and half female to more female, a red flag. For other wildlife, such as orca whales, we do not know: they are hard to catch and study. Both answers to \"are the critters having problems?\" were right: yes for some species, we do not know for others.",
 quote:"you'll start seeing populations of frogs and some fish and alligators changing from being like half male, half female to more female in those places where estrogen levels are higher, we see that. That's like a red flag",
 note:"The vulture (diclofenac) and fathead minnow (ethinyl estradiol) examples in L13-036 and the student discussion guide were not raised in class on 10/13.",
 cite:"Discussion-Guide-Rodriguez-Mozaz-and-Weinberg-Student-2026 copy.pdf (Target/MOA); transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-031", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"reading", skill:"apply", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz",
 concept:"reading-warming-vs-estrogen", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A student asked how we know the shift toward female frogs and alligators is caused by drugs rather than by warmer water. What evidence did she cite?",
 options:[
  {t:"Isolated exposure studies with estrogenic compounds show the same shift", correct:true, why:"Species have been exposed to set doses of hormones or estrogen-like compounds in isolated studies and the shift to female is seen; estrogen levels are also known to be high in those places."},
  {t:"Warming water has been ruled out by temperature studies of the same populations", correct:false, why:"She said more studies are needed and it could be both."},
  {t:"Frogs are not sensitive to temperature", correct:false, why:"She did not rule temperature out: warming waters still need study and it could be both. Her evidence for drugs was isolated exposure studies with estrogenic compounds that produced the shift."},
  {t:"The shift occurs only in cold water", correct:false, why:"The student's question was whether warmer water causes the shift; she said that still needs study and both could contribute, while isolated estrogen-exposure studies show drugs can cause it."},
  {t:"No evidence exists either way", correct:false, why:"The isolated exposure studies are the evidence."}],
 teach:"Her answer to the warming-water question: isolated studies exposed some of these species to certain doses of hormones or estrogen-like compounds and saw the shift toward female, and the estrogen levels in those places are known to be high, so an association is reasonable. Other studies are still needed to test warming, and both could contribute.",
 quote:"we've done isolated studies of some of these critters, some of these species exposed them to certain doses of like hormones, estrogenic type of. Compounds and we do see a shift ... We need to do other studies as well and see if the warming waters is causing that shift. It could be both.",
 cite:"Discussion-Guide-Rodriguez-Mozaz-and-Weinberg-Student-2026 copy.pdf (Target/MOA); transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-032", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"reading", skill:"recall", readingId:"rodriguez-mozaz",
 concept:"reading-antibiotic-resistance", tags:["smith","reading"], source:"both",
 stem:"Besides effects on wildlife, which concern about drugs in the environment did she call a real problem?",
 options:[
  {t:"Bacteria in waters and sediments becoming resistant to antibiotics", correct:true, why:"Improperly disposed antibiotics reach microbes living in sediments, which survive them and become resistant."},
  {t:"Fish accumulating lethal doses of analgesics from the water they live in", correct:false, why:"Not an effect she described."},
  {t:"Drinking water reaching therapeutic concentrations", correct:false, why:"Concentrations are parts per trillion."},
  {t:"Corrosion of water pipes by hormones", correct:false, why:"Her concerns were effects on living things: estrogen shifting wildlife toward female, and antibiotics in the environment selecting resistant bacteria in waters and sediments."},
  {t:"Loss of the Edwards Aquifer", correct:false, why:"The aquifer was named only to define groundwater."}],
 teach:"A big concern she called real: as antibiotics are improperly disposed of and reach the environment, the many microbes living in lake and stream sediments and beach sand survive them and become resistant, so an infection from a cut may be untreatable.",
 quote:"a really big concern, which probably most people know about is the fact that the bacteria in waters and the sediments are becoming resistant to the antibiotics that are out there in the environment, and that's a problem. That is a problem.",
 cite:"Discussion-Guide-Rodriguez-Mozaz-and-Weinberg-Student-2026 copy.pdf (Target/MOA); transcript 10/13"},

/* ---------------- General concepts: immediate/delayed, reversible/irreversible, local/systemic ---------------- */
{id:"L14-033", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"general", skill:"apply",
 concept:"delayed-effect-organophosphate", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A person exposed to an organophosphate pesticide has the expected acute cholinergic effects, recovers, and then develops nerve damage days later. The nerve damage is an example of a(n):",
 options:[
  {t:"Delayed effect", correct:true, why:"Some organophosphates, depending on structure, cause neuropathies that appear days after exposure by affecting different enzymes in the nerves."},
  {t:"Immediate effect", correct:false, why:"The immediate effects are the typical cholinergic ones; the neuropathy comes later."},
  {t:"Local effect", correct:false, why:"Nerve damage is systemic, not at the site of first contact."},
  {t:"Hormetic effect", correct:false, why:"Nothing beneficial occurs."},
  {t:"Dispositional tolerance", correct:false, why:"Tolerance is a reduced response with repeated exposure, not a late-appearing injury."}],
 teach:"Immediate effects are seen right away; delayed effects appear after a lapse of time. Her two extreme examples: diethylstilbestrol (DES) given in the 1950s to maintain pregnancy, with a particular vaginal cancer in the daughters as young adults (a delay of years), and some organophosphate pesticides, which produce the expected immediate effects and then, depending on structure, nerve damage days later from effects on different enzymes in the nerves.",
 quote:"some organophosphate pesticides can have, uh, caused neuropathies because they're affecting um different enzymes in the nerves. OK, so that's a delayed effect by about days, not years.",
 note:"The slide names the delayed neurotoxicant as triorthocresylphosphate (TOCP); in class she said \"some of our organophosphate pesticides\" without naming TOCP.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Immediate vs. Delayed’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-034", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"general", skill:"recall",
 concept:"delayed-effect-des-years", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The two extreme examples of delayed effects differ in their time scale. Diethylstilbestrol (DES) and the organophosphate neuropathy are delayed by, respectively, about:",
 options:[
  {t:"Years and days", correct:true, why:"The DES cancers appeared when the exposed daughters became young adults; the organophosphate neuropathy appears days after exposure."},
  {t:"Days and years", correct:false, why:"DES is the long delay: the cancer showed up only when exposed daughters reached young adulthood, while the organophosphate nerve damage appears days after exposure."},
  {t:"Hours and hours", correct:false, why:"Both are longer than hours."},
  {t:"Years and years", correct:false, why:"The organophosphate neuropathy appears in days."},
  {t:"Minutes and days", correct:false, why:"DES effects took years: the cancers appeared when the exposed daughters became young adults."}],
 teach:"Delayed effects can take quite a lot of time. DES, given in the 1950s to maintain pregnancy, caused a particular vaginal cancer in the female offspring as they became young adults, a delay of years. Some organophosphate pesticides cause nerve damage that appears days after the exposure.",
 quote:"The delayed, very delayed response we saw was a very particular kind of vaginal cancer in their female offspring as those girls became young adults, very delayed exposure, right? ... so that's a delayed effect by about days, not years.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Immediate vs. Delayed’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-035", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"general", skill:"recall",
 concept:"reversibility-regeneration", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What property of a tissue determines whether a toxic injury to it is reversible?",
 options:[
  {t:"Its ability to regenerate", correct:true, why:"The liver can regenerate, so many liver injuries are reversible; differentiated nerve cells cannot divide, so nerve injury is irreversible."},
  {t:"Its blood flow", correct:false, why:"Her liver-versus-nerve contrast is about whether damaged cells can be replaced: liver can regenerate, nerve cells cannot divide. How much blood a tissue receives does not decide recovery."},
  {t:"Its concentration of the toxicant", correct:false, why:"Concentration determines whether injury occurs, not whether the tissue can recover."},
  {t:"Whether the effect was immediate or delayed", correct:false, why:"Timing is a separate general concept."},
  {t:"Whether the exposure was oral", correct:false, why:"The same tissue gives the same answer whatever the route: liver injury can heal by regeneration, nerve injury cannot because nerve cells cannot divide."}],
 teach:"Some toxic effects are reversible and others are not, and the tissue's ability to regenerate decides which. The liver is unique in healing and regenerating itself, so liver injuries can often be reversible (mild injury more than severe chronic injury). Most nerve and CNS cells cannot divide or be regenerated, so nerve damage should be considered irreversible. Cancer and birth defects are also irreversible. One exposure can produce both kinds of injury: \"So sometimes you get both.\"",
 quote:"the liver is really unique. It is able to heal itself, to regenerate oftentimes when it becomes damaged ... most of our, uh, nerves or CNS cells, they cannot be, uh, their injury is permanent. They can't divide. They can't be regenerated.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Reversible vs. Irreversible’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-036", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"general", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cancer-birth-defects-irreversible", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A cancer is removed by surgery and chemotherapy. Why is cancer still classed as an irreversible toxic effect?",
 options:[
  {t:"The cancerous cells were killed, not returned to normal", correct:true, why:"Treatment removes or kills the cells; the cells that became cancerous never reverted."},
  {t:"Cancer cannot be treated", correct:false, why:"It can be cut out, irradiated, or killed with chemotherapy."},
  {t:"Cancer always recurs", correct:false, why:"Irreversibility is about the cells, not the outcome: even when a cancer is cured, the cells that became cancerous were killed or removed, never switched back to normal."},
  {t:"The liver cannot regenerate after surgery or chemotherapy", correct:false, why:"The liver's regeneration is the example of reversibility, not of cancer."},
  {t:"Cancer is a local effect", correct:false, why:"Local versus systemic describes where an agent acts relative to first contact; irreversibility is whether injured cells return to normal, and cancerous cells do not."}],
 teach:"Cancer and birth defects are considered irreversible. A cancer may be cut out, killed with x-rays or chemotherapy, but the cells that became cancerous did not switch back to normal; they were killed. A birth defect in limb or body formation during fetal development cannot be fixed.",
 quote:"that doesn't mean that the tissues, those cells that became cancerous, they didn't, those cancerous cells didn't switch back to being normal, right? We killed them. We got rid of them, OK.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Reversible vs. Irreversible’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-037", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"general", skill:"tell",
 concept:"local-vs-systemic", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Chlorine gas damages the lungs, eyes and nasal mucosa on contact. Why does a highly reactive agent like this tend to produce a local rather than a systemic effect?",
 options:[
  {t:"It reacts at the site of first contact and is often never absorbed", correct:true, why:"Very reactive compounds such as chlorine gas and acids act where they first touch the body and often do not get the chance to go systemic."},
  {t:"It is absorbed too fast to act locally", correct:false, why:"The opposite: it reacts before absorption."},
  {t:"It cannot cross any membrane", correct:false, why:"The point is reactivity at the surface, not membrane crossing."},
  {t:"Most toxins act locally, and chlorine gas is a typical example of that rule", correct:false, why:"Most toxins cause systemic effects; only the highly reactive ones stay local."},
  {t:"The lungs have no blood supply", correct:false, why:"The lungs have ample blood supply; the chlorine reacts before it is absorbed."}],
 teach:"A local effect occurs at the site of first contact: acid splashed on the skin, chlorine gas in the lungs, eyes and nasal mucosa. A systemic effect requires absorption at the site of entry and distribution into the circulation. Most toxins cause systemic effects unless they are highly reactive; highly reactive agents act locally and often never go systemic.",
 quote:"Most toxins, they cause systemic effects unless they are highly reactive. So are very reactive compounds like chlorine gas, like acids, they will cause that, that the local effect. They often will not have a chance to go systemic.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Local vs. Systemic’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-038", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"general", skill:"apply",
 concept:"tetraethyl-lead-local-and-systemic", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A toxicologist got a few drops of tetraethyl lead on her glove; the compound burned through to her skin, and days later she died of neurological effects. What does this case illustrate?",
 options:[
  {t:"A poison with both a local and a systemic effect", correct:true, why:"The burn on the hand was the local effect; absorption through the burned skin and neurotoxicity was the systemic effect."},
  {t:"A purely local effect", correct:false, why:"Her death from neurotoxicity was systemic."},
  {t:"An indirect systemic effect of a burn, through fluid loss", correct:false, why:"Here the compound itself was absorbed and acted on the nervous system; the acid-burn fluid loss is the indirect example."},
  {t:"Dispositional tolerance", correct:false, why:"Tolerance develops with repeated exposure that lowers the amount reaching the site of effect; this was one accident with a few drops, and it was fatal."},
  {t:"Selective toxicity", correct:false, why:"Only one species is involved."}],
 teach:"A few poisons have both local and systemic effects. Tetraethyl lead is her example: drops on a glove reached the skin, burned it (local), were absorbed through the burned skin, and killed her days later by neurotoxicity (systemic). Proper protective equipment matters with such a compound.",
 quote:"they found a burn mark on her hand, where that compound had somehow gotten through the glove, burned her skin. It was absorbed through that burned skin, and then affected her central nervous system and killed her. So a neurotoxin, definitely.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Local vs. Systemic’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-039", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"general", skill:"apply",
 concept:"acid-burn-indirect-systemic", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"An acid burn over a large body surface area leads to kidney problems. How does a local burn produce this systemic effect?",
 options:[
  {t:"Fluid leaks through the damaged skin, blood volume is disturbed, and the kidneys suffer", correct:true, why:"Like a thermal burn, the skin can no longer hold fluids in; the fluid and volume disturbance is the indirect systemic effect."},
  {t:"The acid is absorbed through the damaged skin into the blood and attacks the kidney directly", correct:false, why:"The systemic effect is indirect, through fluid loss, not through absorbed acid."},
  {t:"The burn releases chlorine gas", correct:false, why:"Chlorine gas was her separate example of a reactive agent acting locally; the acid burn's systemic harm comes from fluid leaking through skin that can no longer hold it in."},
  {t:"The acid induces liver enzymes", correct:false, why:"Enzyme changes belong to ADME-based ideas such as dispositional tolerance; the burn's systemic harm comes from fluid loss through damaged skin, which strains blood volume and the kidneys."},
  {t:"Only infection can make a burn systemic", correct:false, why:"Infection is a separate risk she set aside; the fluid problem is the indirect systemic effect."}],
 teach:"Some poisons cause indirect systemic effects. An acid burn over a significant body surface area behaves like a thermal burn: the skin can no longer hold fluids in, blood volume is disturbed, and people can end up with bad kidney problems as the body fights to keep fluid, apart from the risk of infection. A burned patient must be looked at systemically, not only for skin healing.",
 quote:"you run the risk of having your uh blood volume really messed up, cuz you're leaking fluids ... People can end up with some really bad kidney problems because your body's fighting to keep the fluids in you, OK? So that's a systemic effect that can happen with like an acid burn, just like with a thermal burn.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Local vs. Systemic’; transcript 10/13"},

/* ---------------- Chemical interactions ---------------- */
{id:"L14-040", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"interact", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"interaction-types", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of the following are the kinds of interaction that can occur between the compounds a person is exposed to at the same time? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Additive", correct:true, why:"Additive is one of the four interaction types on the slide: compounds taken in together can change each other's absorption, protein binding, biotransformation or excretion, so the combined response may be increased or decreased."},
  {t:"Synergistic", correct:true, why:"Synergistic is one of the four interaction types on the slide: compounds taken in together can change each other's absorption, protein binding, biotransformation or excretion, so the combined response may be increased or decreased."},
  {t:"Potentiation", correct:true, why:"Potentiation is one of the four interaction types on the slide: compounds taken in together can change each other's absorption, protein binding, biotransformation or excretion, so the combined response may be increased or decreased."},
  {t:"Antagonistic", correct:true, why:"Antagonistic is one of the four interaction types on the slide: compounds taken in together can change each other's absorption, protein binding, biotransformation or excretion, so the combined response may be increased or decreased."},
  {t:"Dispositional", correct:false, why:"Dispositional describes a type of tolerance, not an interaction between compounds."}],
 teach:"People are rarely exposed to one thing at a time: contaminated or additive-laden foods, drugs, hobbies and the workplace all contribute. Each exposure probably has its own mechanism and target, and when they are combined the compounds can interact additively, synergistically, by potentiation or antagonistically, which makes the combined outcome hard to predict.",
 quote:"You could have additive or synergistic or potentiation or antagonistic type of interactions going on, which makes it really hard to predict how bad is it going to be for for people or critters, OK.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Chemical Interactions’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-041", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"interact", skill:"apply",
 concept:"risk-assessment-multiple-exposures", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A risk assessor knows the compounds at a polluted site, their concentrations, and how people use the site. Why is it still hard to put a number on the risk?",
 options:[
  {t:"The interactions among the compounds cannot be quantified", correct:true, why:"Her former work: additive, synergistic, potentiating and antagonistic interactions among the exposures are very hard to quantify."},
  {t:"Concentrations in the soil, water and air cannot be measured accurately", correct:false, why:"Sampling measures the concentrations in ground, water or air."},
  {t:"The NOAEL cannot be found for any compound", correct:false, why:"Animal studies supply NOAELs for individual compounds."},
  {t:"People never visit polluted sites", correct:false, why:"Whether people walk across, live on or work at the site is part of the assessment."},
  {t:"Risk assessment is not done for pollution", correct:false, why:"That is exactly where it is done."}],
 teach:"Risk assessment of a polluted site means sampling it, learning which compounds are there and at what concentrations, working out how people interact with the site (walking across, living or working there), and then calculating how risky the whole thing is. The number is hard to get because the interactions among multiple compounds with different mechanisms cannot really be quantified.",
 quote:"It's very hard to get a number that will quantify the risk for being in that place with all those multiple exposures, OK. The reason why it's hard to quantify is it's really hard to quantify these interactions, additive, synergism, potentiation, and antagonism.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Chemical Interactions’; transcript 10/13"},

/* ---------------- Tolerance ---------------- */
{id:"L14-042", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"tolerance", skill:"tell",
 concept:"dispositional-vs-reduced-responsiveness", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What separates dispositional tolerance from reduced-responsiveness tolerance?",
 options:[
  {t:"Dispositional is ADME-based; reduced responsiveness is pharmacodynamic", correct:true, why:"Her summary: dispositional tolerance lowers the amount of toxicant reaching its site; reduced responsiveness is the target itself responding less, such as receptor down-regulation."},
  {t:"Dispositional is seen with drugs; reduced responsiveness only with pollutants", correct:false, why:"Both occur with medications and with environmental exposures."},
  {t:"Dispositional needs a single exposure; reduced responsiveness needs repeated exposure", correct:false, why:"Both kinds develop with repeated exposure."},
  {t:"Dispositional is irreversible; reduced responsiveness is reversible", correct:false, why:"What separates them is where the change happens: in ADME (less compound reaching the site) versus at the target (fewer receptors); reversibility is not part of either definition."},
  {t:"They are the same mechanism with different names", correct:false, why:"They act at different places: dispositional tolerance changes how much compound reaches the site (carbon tetrachloride, cadmium); reduced responsiveness changes the target itself, such as receptor down-regulation."}],
 teach:"Tolerance is decreased responsiveness to a toxic effect after prior exposure; it happens with medications (opioids) and with some environmental or occupational exposures. Two mechanisms: dispositional tolerance, which is ADME-based (less toxicant reaches the site of the toxic effect), and reduced responsiveness, which is pharmacodynamic (the target, such as a receptor population, responds less).",
 quote:"you can see how the dispositional is admi-based and the reduced responsiveness is pharmacodynamically based, right? Dealing with receptors.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Tolerance’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-043", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"tolerance", skill:"apply",
 concept:"carbon-tetrachloride-inhibits-own-metabolism", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Carbon tetrachloride is metabolized to a more toxic product. With repeated exposure, why does tolerance develop?",
 options:[
  {t:"It inhibits its own metabolism, so less of the toxic metabolite forms", correct:true, why:"Her surprising point: carbon tetrachloride inhibits its own metabolism rather than inducing it, partly protecting against the repeated exposure."},
  {t:"It induces the enzymes that metabolize it, so the metabolite is cleared faster", correct:false, why:"That is what she said students would expect; carbon tetrachloride does the opposite."},
  {t:"Its receptors are down-regulated", correct:false, why:"That would be reduced-responsiveness tolerance; this example is dispositional."},
  {t:"It induces a metal-carrying protein", correct:false, why:"Carrier induction is how cadmium is cleared; carbon tetrachloride's tolerance comes from blocking the enzymes that turn it into its nastier metabolite."},
  {t:"It is no longer absorbed", correct:false, why:"Carbon tetrachloride is still taken in; what changes is that it inhibits the enzymes that would convert it to the nastier metabolite."}],
 teach:"Dispositional tolerance, example one: carbon tetrachloride is metabolized by the body to something even nastier, but repeated exposure to carbon tetrachloride inhibits the enzymes that do its own metabolism, so some of it is never converted to the worse metabolite. It is the reverse of the induction most people would expect.",
 quote:"You were probably thinking, oh, carbon tet will induce the enzymes that do its metabolism, but no, for carbontet, it inhibits its own metabolism. It kind of protects you from that repeated exposure, sort of, OK.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Tolerance’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-044", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"tolerance", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cadmium-induces-carrier", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"How does cadmium produce dispositional tolerance?",
 options:[
  {t:"It induces the metal-carrying protein that helps the kidneys eliminate it", correct:true, why:"Like other metals, cadmium is carried by a metal-carrying protein that aids elimination; certain valences of cadmium induce that protein's expression."},
  {t:"It inhibits the enzymes responsible for its own metabolism to a toxic metabolite", correct:false, why:"Self-inhibition of metabolism is carbon tetrachloride's mechanism; cadmium, a metal, is instead carried out through the kidneys by a carrier whose expression it induces."},
  {t:"It down-regulates its receptors", correct:false, why:"Receptor down-regulation is reduced-responsiveness tolerance, not dispositional."},
  {t:"It is converted to a less toxic valence", correct:false, why:"Valence affects induction, not conversion of the metal."},
  {t:"It is stored permanently in bone", correct:false, why:"Her mechanism moves cadmium out of the body, not into storage: the induced metal carrier helps the kidneys eliminate it."}],
 teach:"Dispositional tolerance, example two: cadmium, a common environmental contaminant, is a metal carried in the body by a metal-carrying protein that helps eliminate it through the kidneys. Certain valences of cadmium induce expression of that carrier, so more cadmium is cleared with repeated exposure.",
 quote:"it's often carried in the body by a particular metal carrying enzyme, and if that metal carrying enzyme can grab that cadmium, it'll help you eliminate it via the kidneys. Cadmium, especially um certain valences of cadmium, can induce the expression of that metal carrying enzyme and help you clear it as well.",
 note:"She called the carrier a \"metal carrying enzyme\"; the question says metal-carrying protein.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Tolerance’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-045", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"tolerance", skill:"apply",
 concept:"reduced-responsiveness-downregulation", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient on a medication for months finds it works less well at the same dose. Which type of tolerance, by which mechanism, fits?",
 options:[
  {t:"Reduced responsiveness, by down-regulation of the target receptors", correct:true, why:"With repeated exposure the target down-regulates and the tissue responds less."},
  {t:"Dispositional tolerance, by induction of a carrier protein that clears the drug", correct:false, why:"Carrier induction is the cadmium example of dispositional tolerance."},
  {t:"Dispositional tolerance, by inhibition of metabolism", correct:false, why:"Inhibition of its own metabolism is carbon tetrachloride's way of reducing a toxic metabolite; a medication losing effect at the same dose fits the target down-regulating."},
  {t:"Selective toxicity", correct:false, why:"Selective toxicity is about species, not repeated dosing."},
  {t:"An idiosyncratic reaction", correct:false, why:"Idiosyncratic means unexplained; this mechanism is explained."}],
 teach:"Reduced-responsiveness tolerance: with repeated exposure the target of the compound down-regulates, for instance fewer receptors, so the tissue responds less. A medication that gradually stops working as well can reflect down-regulation of the receptors it targets.",
 quote:"If you give a person a medication over time and it starts to not be working as well, you could be having reduced responsiveness because of a down regulation of the receptors that that medication is targeting, OK.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Tolerance’; transcript 10/13"},

/* ---------------- Variations in effect ---------------- */
{id:"L14-046", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"variation", skill:"tell",
 concept:"selective-toxicity-vs-species-differences", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A compound is lethal to one species and harmless to another. Which term applies?",
 options:[
  {t:"Selective toxicity", correct:true, why:"Lethal to one species but not to another is the definition of selective toxicity."},
  {t:"Species differences", correct:false, why:"Species differences means both species respond but differ qualitatively or quantitatively in how."},
  {t:"Individual differences", correct:false, why:"Individual differences are within one species."},
  {t:"Idiosyncratic toxicity", correct:false, why:"Idiosyncratic means the reason is unknown."},
  {t:"Off-target toxicity", correct:false, why:"On- and off-target describe receptors, not species."}],
 teach:"Toxicologists use specific terms for variation in effect. Selective toxicity: an exposure is lethal to one species but not to another. Species differences: two species are both sensitive, but differ qualitatively and/or quantitatively in how sensitive they are and how they respond. Individual differences: people within one species respond differently, for genetic reasons.",
 quote:"One of the terms is what we call selective toxicity. This means that an exposure could be lethal to one species, but not to another, OK. Another term that we use in the world of toxicology is species differences ... there's either a qualitative and or a quantitative difference in the way two different species respond.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Variations in Effect’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-047", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"variation", skill:"apply",
 concept:"arsenic-no-animal-model", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Arsenic in drinking water causes a skin cancer on the palms and soles in humans, but rats, mice and guinea pigs given the same water do not develop it. What problem does this illustrate?",
 options:[
  {t:"Species differences make animal results hard to extrapolate to humans", correct:true, why:"No animal model reproduces the human response, so the mechanism cannot be studied, and an animal study alone would have wrongly called the arsenic safe."},
  {t:"Arsenic is not toxic to humans", correct:false, why:"The human cancers are the observed fact."},
  {t:"Rodents metabolize arsenic to a safer form before it can reach the skin", correct:false, why:"She said the mechanism is unknown; no explanation was given."},
  {t:"Individual genetic differences within rodents", correct:false, why:"The difference is between species, not within one."},
  {t:"Arsenic is a local toxicant only", correct:false, why:"It acts on the skin after being drunk, a systemic effect."}],
 teach:"Species differences raise the question of extrapolation. Arsenic in groundwater causes a bizarre skin cancer on the palms and soles of exposed humans, but no rodent or other animal tested reproduces it, so the mechanism cannot be worked out. Had the human cases not already been observed, animal studies would have declared the arsenic safe. Choosing the right animal to predict a drug's effects in humans is the same problem.",
 quote:"We have tried and tried to find an animal that we could expose to drinking water that has arsenic in it ... We can't find an animal model that um mimics what happens to humans when we're exposed to arsenic in water, OK?",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Variations in Effect’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-048", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"variation", skill:"recall",
 concept:"individual-differences-genetics", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Two people take the same medication and one has a side effect the other does not. Where does she say the explanation is found?",
 options:[
  {t:"In the genetics of each individual", correct:true, why:"Individual differences within a species come down to genotype, which pharmacogenomics studies."},
  {t:"In species differences", correct:false, why:"Both people are the same species."},
  {t:"In the route of exposure", correct:false, why:"Route is a principle of exposure, not the explanation for within-species variation."},
  {t:"In selective toxicity", correct:false, why:"Selective toxicity compares species."},
  {t:"In dispositional tolerance developed over time", correct:false, why:"Tolerance is about repeated exposure, not two people's first responses."}],
 teach:"Individual differences: people within one species respond to the same medication or environmental exposure in different ways, and the answer lies in each person's genetics. For some medications the genotypes are understood, for others not yet; this is the reason for the pharmacogenomics lectures at the end of her block.",
 quote:"Intraspecies differences, individual differences within a species to a drug or to an environmental exposure, is going to be found. The answer to why is going to be found in the genetics of each individual.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Variations in Effect’; transcript 10/13"},

/* ---------------- On-target and off-target ---------------- */
{id:"L14-049", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"ontarget", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"endpoint-categories", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Toxic endpoints may be described in which of the following ways? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"On-target or off-target", correct:true, why:"The toxicology vocabulary for whether the drug is hitting the receptor it was designed for."},
  {t:"Organ specific or non-organ specific", correct:true, why:"Fibrosis, genotoxicity, cancer and teratogenesis are non-organ specific; lungs, nervous system, liver and kidney are the organ-specific endpoints of the next lecture."},
  {t:"Idiosyncratic", correct:true, why:"Her definition: we do not know why it is happening; it is unexplainable."},
  {t:"Immunologically based, such as allergic reactions", correct:true, why:"Endpoints produced through the immune response are their own category; she said this category will focus mostly on allergic reactions."},
  {t:"Hormetic", correct:false, why:"Hormesis is a dose–response shape, not a category of toxic endpoint."}],
 teach:"Each category answers a different question about a toxic endpoint. On- or off-target: is the drug hitting the receptor it was designed for? Organ specific or not: is the damage tied to one organ, or is the same pathology seen in many? Idiosyncratic: we do not know why it happens. Immunologically based: mostly allergic reactions. One exposure can have several targets, and each response may come from a different mechanism.",
 quote:"toxic endpoints may be defined as on or off target ... The endpoints or the results you see may be non-organ specific, they could be organ specific, they could be idiosyncratic. What does idiosyncratic mean? We don't know why it's happening. It's unexplainable, OK?",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Toxic Endpoints may be…’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-050", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"ontarget", skill:"figure",
 concept:"figure-intended-vs-unintended-tissue", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the Figure 6-1 diagram of adverse drug effects, the left half and the right half show, respectively:",
 options:[
  {t:"The intended tissue and an unintended tissue", correct:true, why:"The left is where we want the drug to act; the right is a tissue where the drug goes but is not wanted."},
  {t:"The parent drug and its metabolite", correct:false, why:"Drug or metabolite activity appears on both sides."},
  {t:"The therapeutic dose and the toxic dose of the drug", correct:false, why:"Dose is one of the reasons listed under the halves, not what divides them."},
  {t:"Agonists and antagonists", correct:false, why:"The figure does not divide by drug class."},
  {t:"Reversible and irreversible effects", correct:false, why:"Reversibility is a different general concept."}],
 teach:"The on-/off-target figure (from the pharmacology textbook, Chapter 6, Figure 6-1) is divided in half: the left is the intended tissue, where the drug is meant to act, and the right is an unintended tissue the drug may also reach. On both sides the drug, or a metabolite of it, finds receptors. On-target means it finds the receptor it was designed for; off-target means it finds other receptors; either can occur in either tissue.",
 quote:"What kind of tissue is being described on the left? Right here The intended tissue, right? So this is where we want this, let's say this is a drug. This is where we want the drug to act in the body, the intended tissue. And on the right here. This is an image of what's going on in an unintended tissue.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘On-target and off-target adverse drug effects’ (Figure 6-1); transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-051", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"ontarget", skill:"tell",
 concept:"on-target-wrong-tissue-naming", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A drug hits the receptor it was designed for, but in a tissue where that action is unwanted. In the toxicology vocabulary this adverse effect is called:",
 options:[
  {t:"On-target, in the wrong tissue", correct:true, why:"It is classically called on-target: the drug hits the receptor it was meant to hit, in the wrong tissue. She said she wishes it were called something else."},
  {t:"Off-target", correct:false, why:"Off-target means the drug finds a receptor it was not intended to find, in either tissue."},
  {t:"Idiosyncratic", correct:false, why:"The mechanism here is known."},
  {t:"Dispositional", correct:false, why:"Dispositional describes a kind of tolerance."},
  {t:"Indirect systemic, as in the acid burn", correct:false, why:"Indirect systemic effects come from a local injury, such as fluid loss through burned skin, not from a drug binding its intended receptor in the wrong tissue."}],
 teach:"On-target: the drug or its metabolite interacts with the receptor it was intended to target. It is still on-target when that receptor is in the wrong tissue, which is the part of the vocabulary she dislikes. Off-target: the drug finds other receptors it was not intended to find, whether in the intended tissue or in another one.",
 quote:"even though the drug is in the wrong tissue, when it's finding the receptor that we wanted it to find, it's still called on target. So this is a part of our toxicology vernacular that I really don't like. I wish we would call it like off target or something",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘On-target and off-target adverse drug effects’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-052", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"ontarget", skill:"recall",
 concept:"on-target-right-tissue-dose", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A drug produces an adverse effect by acting on its intended receptor in its intended tissue. The most common reason is:",
 options:[
  {t:"The dose is too high", correct:true, why:"Her rule: an on-target adverse effect in the right tissue usually means too much dose at the receptor."},
  {t:"An enantiomer hit another receptor", correct:false, why:"That is an off-target mechanism (thalidomide)."},
  {t:"The drug crossed the blood-brain barrier", correct:false, why:"That is on-target in the wrong tissue (diphenhydramine)."},
  {t:"The drug blocked a receptor subtype", correct:false, why:"Hitting an unintended receptor subtype, as propranolol does at beta-2 receptors in the airway, is an off-target effect, not an on-target effect in the intended tissue."},
  {t:"The reaction is idiosyncratic", correct:false, why:"The mechanism here is simply overdose."}],
 teach:"On-target adverse effects in the intended tissue come from too high a dose at the receptor, or from chronic activation or inhibition of other effects. Too much dose at the receptor can come from a deliberate overdose, an accidental dosing error, a change in pharmacokinetics (a diseased liver that cannot clear the normal dose, giving blood concentrations that are too high), or a change in receptor number (down-regulated receptors over-hit by the same dose).",
 quote:"Most of the time, if you're having an on target adverse effect in the right tissue, your dose is too high. OK.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slides ‘On-target and off-target adverse drug effects’, ‘On-Target Examples’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-053", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"ontarget", skill:"apply", multi:true,
 concept:"dose-at-receptor-too-high-causes", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"A patient on an unchanged dose of a drug develops an on-target adverse effect. Which of the following could have raised the dose at the receptor? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Liver disease that slows clearance of the drug", correct:true, why:"A change in pharmacokinetics: the normal dose is not cleared and blood concentrations rise."},
  {t:"Down-regulation of the receptors with the dose kept the same", correct:true, why:"A change in receptor number: fewer receptors are over-hit by the same dose."},
  {t:"An accidental dosing error by a healthcare provider", correct:true, why:"A provider's error puts more drug than intended at the intended receptor, and too much dose at the receptor is the usual cause of an on-target adverse effect."},
  {t:"A deliberate overdose by the patient", correct:true, why:"Taking too much on purpose raises the dose at the intended receptor, giving an on-target adverse effect from the drug's own action."},
  {t:"The drug finding a different receptor subtype", correct:false, why:"That is off-target, not a rise in dose at the intended receptor."}],
 teach:"On-target examples: the dose at the receptor is too high through deliberate or accidental dosing errors; through a change in pharmacokinetics, such as a liver that no longer metabolizes and clears the drug so the normal dose yields too-high blood concentrations; or through a change in receptor number, such as down-regulation with the dose unchanged.",
 quote:"Maybe their liver is not working as well anymore, and they can't metabolize and clear the drug ... So you've got, again, too much dose at the target, OK? Also, you could have a change in receptor number. So if you down regulate receptor numbers, keep the dose the same, you may be overhitting those receptors",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘On-Target Examples’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-054", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"ontarget", skill:"drug",
 concept:"diphenhydramine-on-target-wrong-tissue", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Diphenhydramine (Benadryl) relieves allergy symptoms at peripheral histamine receptors and causes drowsiness at histamine receptors in the brain. The drowsiness is a(n):",
 options:[
  {t:"On-target effect in the wrong tissue", correct:true, why:"The same histamine receptor is hit, but in the brain, where it is not wanted."},
  {t:"Off-target effect at an unintended receptor", correct:false, why:"The receptor is the intended one; only the tissue is wrong."},
  {t:"Enantiomer effect", correct:false, why:"An enantiomer effect needs a racemic drug whose second form hits another target, like thalidomide's S form; diphenhydramine's drowsiness comes from the same histamine receptor, in the brain."},
  {t:"Receptor-subtype effect", correct:false, why:"A subtype effect means hitting a different subtype, like beta-2 instead of beta-1 for propranolol; diphenhydramine hits the same histamine receptor it treats allergy with, just in the brain."},
  {t:"Idiosyncratic reaction", correct:false, why:"The mechanism is known."}],
 teach:"Diphenhydramine blocks allergic symptoms by hitting histamine receptors in the periphery. It also crosses into the brain and hits the same histamine receptors there, causing drowsiness. Because the receptor is the intended one and only the tissue is wrong, this is an on-target adverse effect in the wrong tissue. Newer antihistamines such as cetirizine and fexofenadine were tweaked so they do not cross the blood-brain barrier, so they reach the peripheral receptors without the drowsiness. For the receptor class, keep Dr. Gottlieb's Exam 1 point: \"there's only one inverse agonist you need to know. It's gonna be an antihistamine. If you know what loratadine is, you know that's the inverse agonist\".",
 quote:"it causes drowsiness because it's hitting the histamine receptors in the brain, so that's an on target adverse effect in the wrong tissue. When we developed newer antihistamines, we tweaked those molecules, and we made, made them so that they don't cross the blood-brain barrier.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘On-Target Examples’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-055", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"ontarget", skill:"drug",
 concept:"terfenadine-off-target", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Terfenadine (Seldane) was withdrawn from the market. Its desired and undesired effects were, respectively:",
 options:[
  {t:"Peripheral H1 receptor antagonism and inhibition of cardiac potassium channels", correct:true, why:"The antihistamine effect at peripheral H1 receptors; the arrhythmias from an unintended receptor, cardiac potassium channels."},
  {t:"Inhibition of cardiac potassium channels and then peripheral H1 receptor antagonism", correct:false, why:"Terfenadine was made to treat allergy at peripheral H1 receptors; blocking cardiac potassium channels was the unintended off-target effect behind her racing heart."},
  {t:"H1 antagonism in the brain and drowsiness", correct:false, why:"Drowsiness in the brain is the diphenhydramine on-target example."},
  {t:"Beta-1 blockade and asthma", correct:false, why:"That pair is propranolol, where beta-1 blockade is the wanted effect and asthma comes from beta-2; terfenadine's pair is H1 antagonism and potassium-channel inhibition."},
  {t:"Anti-nausea effect and teratogenesis", correct:false, why:"That pair is thalidomide's two enantiomers; terfenadine's wanted effect was allergy relief at H1 receptors and its unwanted one was heart rhythm problems."}],
 teach:"Off-target, example one: a drug hits an unintended receptor. Terfenadine (Seldane) treated allergies at peripheral H1 receptors but inhibited cardiac potassium channels, causing cardiac problems; it is no longer on the market. Her own racing heart in high school came from Seldane, worse on grapefruit mornings: grapefruit blocked its metabolism and clearance, raising blood levels and blocking more potassium channels.",
 quote:"we were having cardiac problems with that drug because it was inhibiting cardiac potassium channels ... I wasn't metabolizing and clearing selane because of the grapefruit. I was having increased levels of selane in my blood, so blocking even more of my potassium channels.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Off-Target Examples’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-056", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"ontarget", skill:"drug",
 concept:"thalidomide-enantiomers", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Racemic thalidomide was used for nausea in pregnancy and caused birth defects. Which statement about its enantiomers is correct?",
 options:[
  {t:"The R-enantiomer treats nausea; the S-enantiomer is a potent teratogen", correct:true, why:"The drug was taken as a racemic mixture, so people got both enantiomers."},
  {t:"The S-enantiomer treats nausea; the R-enantiomer is the potent teratogen", correct:false, why:"The teratogen is the S form and the R form helped nausea; giving the racemic mixture is what exposed pregnant women to the S form."},
  {t:"Both enantiomers are teratogens", correct:false, why:"Only the S-enantiomer is the potent teratogen."},
  {t:"Only the R-enantiomer was ever given", correct:false, why:"It was given as a racemic mixture of R and S."},
  {t:"Thalidomide is no longer used for anything", correct:false, why:"It is used today in oncology."}],
 teach:"Off-target, example two: an enantiomer hits an unintended receptor. Thalidomide is racemic; the R-enantiomer helped with nausea and the S-enantiomer is a potent teratogen, and people took both. It was never officially on the market in the 1950s and is used today in oncology.",
 quote:"it's the Rnanammer that was good at helping with nausea, but the Senanammer was a very potent teratogen, OK. Do you guys know if we use, uh, thalidomide anymore therapeutically? ... yeah, we use thalidomide now in oncology.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Off-Target Examples’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-057", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"ontarget", skill:"drug",
 concept:"propranolol-subtype-off-target", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Propranolol lowers heart rate by blocking beta-1 adrenergic receptors in the heart and can worsen asthma. The asthma exacerbation is an off-target effect of which kind?",
 options:[
  {t:"An unintended receptor subtype: beta-2 receptors in airway smooth muscle", correct:true, why:"The beta-blocker example is about receptor subtypes, beta-1 versus beta-2."},
  {t:"An enantiomer hitting another receptor", correct:false, why:"Enantiomer effects need a racemic drug with a second form, like thalidomide; propranolol's asthma comes from blocking a second receptor subtype, beta-2."},
  {t:"The intended receptor, beta-1, in the wrong tissue, the airway smooth muscle", correct:false, why:"Beta-2 is a different subtype from the intended beta-1, so it is off-target."},
  {t:"Dose too high at beta-1 receptors", correct:false, why:"That would be on-target."},
  {t:"Inhibition of cardiac potassium channels", correct:false, why:"Potassium-channel block is terfenadine's off-target effect on the heart; propranolol's asthma comes from blocking beta-2 receptors in airway smooth muscle."}],
 teach:"Propranolol is meant to block beta-1 adrenergic receptors in the heart, lowering heart rate. It also blocks beta-2 adrenergic receptors, a different subtype, located in airway smooth muscle, and that is what exacerbates asthma. Because the airway effect comes from a receptor subtype the drug was not designed to hit, it is off-target, not on-target in the wrong tissue.",
 quote:"the asthma exacerbation example with the beta blockers is, it's talking about subtypes of a receptor. So, beta 1 adrenergic receptors versus the beta 2 adrenergic receptors, OK?",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Off-Target Examples’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-058", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"ontarget", skill:"apply",
 concept:"off-target-fix-molecule", tags:["smith"], source:"transcript",
 stem:"A drug class is therapeutically useful but its early members have many unrelated side effects. What do medicinal chemists do about an off-target effect?",
 options:[
  {t:"Change the part of the molecule that hits the other receptor", correct:true, why:"Her account: the lab looks at the molecule, identifies the part interacting with off-target receptors, and alters it so the drug no longer hits them."},
  {t:"Raise the dose until the therapeutic effect dominates the side effects", correct:false, why:"A higher dose would increase both effects."},
  {t:"Give the drug only in the intended tissue", correct:false, why:"Off-target effects can occur in the intended tissue too."},
  {t:"Accept the side effects as idiosyncratic", correct:false, why:"Off-target effects have a known mechanism: another receptor."},
  {t:"Switch to the racemic mixture", correct:false, why:"A racemic mixture can add an unwanted enantiomer (thalidomide)."}],
 teach:"A drug is off-target, or unclean, when some molecular feature interacts with receptors the designers did not intend. Once a class shows many side effects, the lab goes back to the molecule, identifies the part hitting the other receptor, and changes it so the next generation avoids the off-target receptors. The newer non-drowsy antihistamines are the on-target version of the same strategy.",
 quote:"People in the lab will go back and look at that molecule of a drug and say, oh, it's this part of the molecule that's hitting a different receptor. Let's see if we can change that part of the drug. So that maybe it won't hit the off target receptors.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘On-target and off-target adverse drug effects’; transcript 10/13"},

/* ---------------- Non-organ-specific endpoints ---------------- */
{id:"L14-059", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"nonorgan", skill:"recall",
 concept:"fibrosis-pathogenesis", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Fibrosis causes loss of organ function through:",
 options:[
  {t:"Deposition of collagen and matrix proteins that stiffen the tissue", correct:true, why:"Fibrotic tissue is stiff: lungs lose flexibility, and in the liver the deposits interfere with bile ducts and hepatocyte function."},
  {t:"Death of the whole organ at once from a single exposure to the compound", correct:false, why:"Function is lost as stiff tissue replaces working tissue."},
  {t:"Cancerous transformation", correct:false, why:"Fibrosis loses function by laying down stiff collagen and matrix proteins, not by cells turning cancerous; cancer is a separate non-organ-specific endpoint."},
  {t:"Loss of the blood-brain barrier", correct:false, why:"Fibrosis harms through stiff deposits in tissues such as lung and liver; the blood-brain barrier came up only for antihistamines and drowsiness."},
  {t:"Receptor down-regulation", correct:false, why:"That is reduced-responsiveness tolerance."}],
 teach:"Fibrosis: some compounds make the body lay down collagen and other extracellular matrix proteins in a tissue. Fibrotic tissue is stiff, so the lungs cannot flex and in the liver the deposits get in the way of bile ducts and hepatocyte function; organ function is lost as the tissue is destroyed.",
 quote:"some compounds can cause your body to lay down collagen and other extracellular matrix proteins in various tissues, and that's your fibrotic tissue being created. Fibrotic tissue is stiff.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Non-Organ Specific: Fibrosis’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-060", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"nonorgan", skill:"drug", multi:true,
 concept:"pulmonary-fibrosis-agents", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Which of the following are the agents she named as causes of pulmonary fibrosis? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Amiodarone", correct:true, why:"A cardiac (antiarrhythmic) medication; it also causes liver fibrosis."},
  {t:"Bleomycin", correct:true, why:"A chemotherapy drug; with amiodarone and paraquat it is one of the classic causes of pulmonary fibrosis she listed."},
  {t:"Paraquat", correct:true, why:"An herbicide, once used in suicide attempts; death from pulmonary fibrosis is long and slow."},
  {t:"Ethanol", correct:false, why:"Ethanol is her example of liver fibrosis (cirrhosis), not lung fibrosis."},
  {t:"Diphenhydramine", correct:false, why:"The antihistamine is the on-target drowsiness example."}],
 teach:"Pulmonary fibrosis occurs in the alveolar interstitium; the classic causes are amiodarone (an antiarrhythmic), bleomycin (a chemotherapeutic) and paraquat (an herbicide). Amiodarone also causes liver fibrosis, and the familiar cause of liver fibrosis, cirrhosis, is ethanol.",
 quote:"classic examples of compounds that cause pulmonary fibrosis are amiodarone. Bleomycin, amiodarone is a cardiac med. Bleomycin is a chemotherapy. Paraquats is actually an herbicide ... something that you are probably very familiar with that causes liver fibrosis is ethanol. We call that cirrhosis of the liver",
 note:"The slide spells the herbicide \"Paraquot\" and she said \"Paraquats\"; the bank uses paraquat.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Non-Organ Specific: Fibrosis’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-061", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"nonorgan", skill:"apply",
 concept:"fibrosis-non-organ-specific-why", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Pulmonary fibrosis and liver cirrhosis occur in different organs. Why is fibrosis still classed as a non-organ-specific toxic endpoint?",
 options:[
  {t:"The same collagen and matrix proteins are laid down whatever the organ", correct:true, why:"Her answer: if you look at the cells and chemistry of fibrotic tissue, lung fibrosis and liver fibrosis look the same."},
  {t:"The same compounds always cause both the lung and the liver forms of fibrosis", correct:false, why:"Bleomycin and paraquat affect the lung; ethanol the liver; only amiodarone was named for both."},
  {t:"Fibrosis spreads from one organ to all the others", correct:false, why:"Non-organ specific does not mean it spreads; it means the fibrotic tissue looks the same, with the same collagen and matrix proteins, in whatever organ it forms."},
  {t:"Fibrosis occurs only in the blood", correct:false, why:"It occurs in tissues such as lung and liver."},
  {t:"It is idiosyncratic", correct:false, why:"The mechanism of fibrosis is known."}],
 teach:"Fibrosis is non-organ specific even though particular compounds target particular organs: whether in the lung or the liver, the fibrotic tissue shows the same collagen and the same extracellular matrix proteins, so the same pathology creates it. Pulmonary fibrosis does not look one way and liver fibrosis another.",
 quote:"if you look at the cells, if you look at the chemistry of that fibrotic tissue, it's the same. So the same pathology goes on to create that fibrosis. So it's not like pulmonary fibrosis looks one way and liver fibrosis looks another.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Non-Organ Specific: Fibrosis’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-062", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"nonorgan", skill:"tell",
 concept:"genotoxicity-vs-mutagenicity", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"What separates mutagenicity from genotoxicity?",
 options:[
  {t:"Mutagenicity is transmissible to offspring; genotoxicity is any DNA damage", correct:true, why:"Genotoxicity is damage to DNA; mutagenicity refers to a transmissible genetic alteration that can be passed down."},
  {t:"Genotoxicity is transmissible to offspring; mutagenicity is confined to one cell", correct:false, why:"Transmissibility is what makes something mutagenic; genotoxicity is the broad term for any damage to DNA, which need not be passed on."},
  {t:"Mutagenicity occurs only in somatic cells", correct:false, why:"A mutation in germ cells is what makes an agent a mutagen."},
  {t:"Genotoxicity always causes cancer", correct:false, why:"Its results can be cancer, teratogenesis or genetically based disorders, not always cancer."},
  {t:"They are the same thing", correct:false, why:"Genotoxicity is the broad term for any DNA damage, in somatic or germ cells; mutagenicity is the narrower case where the change can be passed to offspring."}],
 teach:"Genotoxicity is damage to DNA in general and can occur in somatic cells as well as germ cells. Mutagenicity means the genetic alteration is transmissible: it can be passed down to offspring. If the mutations happen in germ cells, the agent is a mutagen. The results of genotoxicity can be cancer, teratogenesis (birth defects) or genetically based disorders, including some metabolic diseases.",
 quote:"genotoxicity is just talking about damage to your DNA in general. And mutagenicity refers to the fact that it's transmissible. In other words, you can pass that down to your offspring, OK? ... If mutations happen in your only your germ cells, then it's a mutagen",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Non-Organ Specific: Genotoxicity’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-063", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"nonorgan", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"genotoxicity-results", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The results of genotoxicity can include which of the following? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"Cancer", correct:true, why:"Damage to DNA can lead to cancer, the first of her three results of genotoxicity, alongside birth defects and genetically based disorders."},
  {t:"Teratogenesis", correct:true, why:"Damage to DNA can also show up as birth defects, the second of her three results of genotoxicity."},
  {t:"Genetically based disorders", correct:true, why:"Some metabolic diseases have been traced to genetic damage."},
  {t:"Fibrosis", correct:false, why:"Fibrosis is a separate non-organ-specific endpoint, not a result of DNA damage."},
  {t:"Dispositional tolerance", correct:false, why:"Tolerance is a reduced response with repeated exposure."}],
 teach:"Genotoxicity is damage to DNA in general, in somatic cells or germ cells. Because it is damage to the genetic material rather than to one organ, it is non-organ specific, and it can show up in three ways: cancer, teratogenesis (birth defects), or genetically based disorders; some metabolic diseases have been traced to genetic damage. When the damage can be passed to offspring, the agent is a mutagen.",
 quote:"the results of genotoxicity can be bad. You can have cancer, you can have teratogenesis, which is birth defects, or you can have genetically-based disorders. So some of our metabolic diseases have been traced to genetic damage",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Non-Organ Specific: Genotoxicity’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-064", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"nonorgan", skill:"figure",
 concept:"cancer-incidence-table", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"In the cancer incidence table she showed, which cancer has the highest incidence in both men and women, and which are second for each sex?",
 options:[
  {t:"Lung and bronchial cancer; then prostate in men and breast in women", correct:true, why:"On the table lung and bronchial cancer tops both columns, and the second-ranked cancers are prostate in men and breast in women."},
  {t:"Breast cancer in both; then lung", correct:false, why:"Breast is the second-ranked cancer in women."},
  {t:"Colon cancer in both sexes; then prostate in men and breast in women", correct:false, why:"Colon cancer came up only in her example of shared mutations."},
  {t:"Prostate in men and breast in women; then skin", correct:false, why:"Lung and bronchial cancer rank first for both."},
  {t:"Liver cancer in both", correct:false, why:"Liver tumors were the hamster example from 10/9."}],
 teach:"She showed the incidence table (from Harvard reports) to set up a puzzle. Lung and bronchial cancer is highest in both men and women, then prostate in men and breast in women, so cancer clearly occurs more in some organs than others. Yet she calls cancer non-organ specific, because cancers in different organs can share the same cellular pathology, such as the same mutation in some lung and colon cancers.",
 quote:"the lung and bronchial cancer is highest for both men and women, and then for men it's prostate and breast it's women. And then most of the time these same cancers are happening with the same frequency in men versus women.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Non-Specific: Cancer’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-065", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"nonorgan", skill:"apply",
 concept:"cancer-non-organ-specific-why", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Incidence differs by organ, yet cancer is called a non-organ-specific endpoint. Why?",
 options:[
  {t:"The same cellular mechanism can cause cancers in different organs", correct:true, why:"Some lung cancers share the genetic mutation seen in colon cancers; some lung and breast cancers share a mechanism; the pathology at the cell level is the same across organs."},
  {t:"Every organ gets cancer at the same rate once incidence is adjusted for age", correct:false, why:"The table shows rates differ by organ."},
  {t:"Cancer never stays in one organ", correct:false, why:"Non-organ specific is about shared mechanism, not spread: a cancer in the lung can arise by the same cellular mechanism as one in the colon or breast."},
  {t:"Cancer has no known mechanism", correct:false, why:"The shared mechanisms are the point."},
  {t:"Cancer is caused only by fibrosis", correct:false, why:"Fibrosis is a separate endpoint."}],
 teach:"Cancer can happen in any organ, more likely in some than others. It is non-organ specific because, at the cellular level, the pathology causing a cancer in one organ can be the same as in another: some lung cancers carry the same genetic mutation as colon cancers; some lung cancers share mechanisms with breast cancers. Not always: \"That is not always the case, but we do see this happening.\"",
 quote:"classic example would be some lung cancers are caused by the same genetic mutation as you will see in colon cancers, OK. Some Some lung cancers are what we'll also see in some breast cancers, so at the cellular level, the pathology is the same",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Non-Specific: Cancer’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-066", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"nonorgan", skill:"recall", lowYield:true,
 concept:"multistage-carcinogenesis", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The multistage model of carcinogenesis holds that cancer usually requires:",
 options:[
  {t:"More than one mutation", correct:true, why:"Her one-sentence summary of the slide she skipped: it would be rare for a single mutation to go all the way to cancer."},
  {t:"A single mutation in one gene", correct:false, why:"The model exists to explain why one mutation is rarely enough."},
  {t:"Fibrosis first", correct:false, why:"Fibrosis is a separate endpoint."},
  {t:"Exposure in utero", correct:false, why:"That is teratogenesis."},
  {t:"A receptor subtype", correct:false, why:"Receptor subtypes belong to the off-target discussion."}],
 teach:"The multistage model of carcinogenesis (initiation, promotion, progression on the slide) says it takes more than one mutation to end up with cancer; one mutation going all the way to cancer would be rare. She skipped the slide for time and pointed to the Casarett and Doull text on AccessPharmacy.",
 quote:"Basically it's saying that Sorry. We think it takes more than one mutation to end up with somebody having cancer. It would be rare to have just one mutation going all the way to cancer. The multi-stage model of carcinogenesis helps explain that. OK. We're gonna skip that today.",
 note:"Skipped in class on 10/13 (\"we're running a little bit short on time\"); only the one-sentence summary was spoken. The slide's initiation, promotion, progression and complete-carcinogen definitions were not taught. Low yield.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Non-Organ Specific: Cancer’ (multistage model); transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-067", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"nonorgan", skill:"apply",
 concept:"teratogen-time-of-exposure", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Hamsters given a retinoid at different days of gestation produce offspring with different birth defects (small eyes, exencephaly, spina bifida, cleft palate, shortened forelimbs, tail defects). What does this show about teratogens?",
 options:[
  {t:"The time of exposure during gestation determines the type of defect", correct:true, why:"The limb defects were expected; the other defects appeared when the mothers were dosed at other times in gestation."},
  {t:"Retinoids damage only the limbs, whatever the day of gestation the mother is dosed", correct:false, why:"That was the expectation; the study showed other defects at other times."},
  {t:"The dose determines the organ affected", correct:false, why:"Timing, not dose, set the type of defect in this study."},
  {t:"Hamsters are a poor model for birth defects", correct:false, why:"The hamster data are the evidence she used."},
  {t:"Teratogens act only in late gestation", correct:false, why:"Early exposure gave eye and neural tube defects."}],
 teach:"Teratogenesis is non-organ specific because the same exposure can produce different effects in the developing fetus depending on when in gestation the mother is exposed. In hamsters, retinoids were known to cause limb deformations, but dosing the mothers at different gestation days produced microphthalmia (little eyes), exencephaly, spina bifida, cleft palate (twice on the timeline), forelimb shortening and tail effects.",
 quote:"We didn't expect some of these other birth defects to be happening with this retinoid exposure in hamsters until we started giving it to the mothers at different times in their gestation. So this is a classic example of why a teratogen. is not necessarily organ specific.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Teratogenesis Time of exposure’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-068", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"nonorgan", skill:"apply",
 concept:"different-compounds-same-defect", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"Methanol given to hamster mothers on gestation day 7 and TCDD given on gestation day 11 both produce cleft palate. What point does this make?",
 options:[
  {t:"Different compounds can cause the same defect, with the fetus sensitive at different times", correct:true, why:"Her reading of the table of palate deformities: four compounds, four peak gestation days, one defect."},
  {t:"Methanol and TCDD share one mechanism of action on the developing palate at the same stage", correct:false, why:"The point is the shared outcome, not a shared mechanism: the two compounds peak on different days (7 and 11), and the table says nothing about how each acts on the palate."},
  {t:"Cleft palate occurs only on day 7", correct:false, why:"Each compound had its own peak day."},
  {t:"TCDD is not a teratogen", correct:false, why:"It produced the cleft palate."},
  {t:"Dose, not timing, explains the table", correct:false, why:"The table lists the gestation day of peak effect for each compound."}],
 teach:"The table adapted from Casarett and Doull lists the gestation day of peak palate deformity in hamsters for methanol (day 7), TCDD (day 11), 2,4,5-trichloroacetic acid (day 12) and dexamethasone (day 13): different compounds cause the same teratogenic effect, and the fetus may be sensitive to each at a different time of gestation.",
 quote:"if you give methanol at 7 days of gestation to the mother. You end up with a a palate deformity or cleft palate. If you give TCDD at 11 days of gestation, you get the same deformity, the, the cleft palate. OK? So this is just to show you that different compounds can cause the same teratogenic effect",
 note:"She called the third compound \"trichloric acid\"; the slide lists 2,4,5-trichloroacetic acid.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Teratogenesis with different compounds’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-069", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"nonorgan", skill:"recall",
 concept:"teratogen-dose-maternal", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"For a teratogen, what determines the dose the fetus receives?",
 options:[
  {t:"The mother must take in enough for it to pass through to the baby", correct:true, why:"Her one-line summary of the dose slide: fetal exposure comes through the mother's exposure and the compound's passage to the fetus."},
  {t:"The fetus is dosed directly, independent of the mother's absorption and metabolism", correct:false, why:"The fetus is exposed through the mother's exposure."},
  {t:"The dose does not matter for teratogens", correct:false, why:"Dose still matters; there is a dose continuum."},
  {t:"Only the father's exposure matters", correct:false, why:"Male fertility is a labeling point, not the route of fetal dose."},
  {t:"Only the time of exposure matters", correct:false, why:"Timing sets the type of defect; dose sets whether enough reaches the fetus."}],
 teach:"Teratogenesis is the induction of birth defects in the fetus. The fetus is exposed through the mother's exposure, so fetal exposure depends on the mother's absorption, distribution, metabolism and excretion and on whether the compound crosses the placenta. Dose means the mother has to get enough of the compound for it to pass through to the baby. She skipped the rest of the dose slide.",
 quote:"dose in teratogenicity means the mother has to get enough of the compound for it to pass through into the baby, OK? And, uh, I, I would talk about a little bit more detail there, but we'll skip that.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slides ‘Non-Organ Specific: Teratogenesis’, ‘Teratogenesis Dose’; transcript 10/13"},

{id:"L14-070", lecture:"L14", prof:"Smith", tier:"new", topic:"L14", sub:"nonorgan", skill:"recall", multi:true,
 concept:"fda-pregnancy-lactation-labeling", tags:["smith"], source:"both",
 stem:"The FDA changed its pregnancy labeling about five years ago. Which kinds of information does the labeling now carry? Select all that apply.",
 options:[
  {t:"How teratogenic the drug is", correct:true, why:"The information the old labeling scale carried, kept in the new labeling."},
  {t:"Effects on fertility, including male fertility", correct:true, why:"New: a drug may affect sperm count or viability and contribute to teratogenicity."},
  {t:"Whether a lactating mother should take the drug", correct:true, why:"New: children should not ingest certain medications in breast milk."},
  {t:"The drug's therapeutic index", correct:false, why:"The labeling change added fertility and lactation to the teratogenicity information; the therapeutic index is a dose-response ratio, not a pregnancy or lactation item."},
  {t:"The drug's margin of exposure", correct:false, why:"A pollutant value, not a drug label item."}],
 teach:"The FDA used to rank drugs on a scale from known to cause birth defects to not; about five years ago it changed the labeling. Besides a drug's teratogenicity, the labeling now includes its effects on fertility, including male fertility (sperm count and viability, which can contribute to teratogenicity), and whether a nursing mother should take it, since children should not ingest certain medications in breast milk.",
 quote:"FDA changed the labeling to include not just information on a drug's stratogenicity, but they now include information on fertility, especially male fertility, and then also put in the same labeling place information on whether or not lactating women should take that medication.",
 cite:"Toxic Responses Students Fall 2026 WCAG21A.pdf slide ‘Teratogenesis Labeling’ (Link to FDA Pregnancy and Lactation Labeling); transcript 10/13"}
);
